var tafsirData_5 = {
  "1": "O you who believe, fulfil contracts — grazing livestock made lawful except what will be recited — hunting not lawful while in ihram — Allah judges what He wills. Ya ayyuha alladhina amanu awfu bil-uqud — Fulfil contracts — uqud — plural of aqd — firm knot — includes contracts with Allah — tawhid, prayer, halal haram — and with people — marriage dowry firm covenant, business, oath, trust returning, inheritance.\n\nUhillat lakum bahimatu al-an'am illa ma yutla alaykum ghayra muhilli as-saydi wa antum hurum — Grazing livestock made lawful except what will be recited — bahimah al-an'am — camels cattle sheep — lawful except carrion blood swine etc recited next verse.\n\nGhayra muhilli as-saydi wa antum hurum — Hunting not lawful while in ihram — ihram sacred state — as earlier Sacred House sanctuary, months sacred.\n\nInnAllaha yahkumu ma yurid — Allah judges what wills — yahkumu — decrees — as Owner dominion gives whom wills (3:26) — judges what wills.\n\nOpening with awfu bil-uqud sets theme Ma'idah — Table Spread — which is about covenants: covenant Israel, covenant Christians, covenant believers.\n\nFulfilling contracts begins with halal food contract — what lawful to eat — and ihram hunting contract — respecting sanctuary.\n\nModern: fulfilling employment contracts, marriage contracts, digital terms honest.\n\nNext do not violate rites pilgrimage sacred months.",
  "2": "O you who believe, do not violate rites of Allah — sacred months, sacrificial animals, garlands, nor those heading to Sacred House seeking bounty and pleasure from Lord — when you exit ihram you may hunt — let not hatred of people who barred you from Sacred Mosque lead you to transgress — help one another in righteousness and piety not in sin and transgression — fear Allah — Allah severe in punishment.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu la tuhillu sha'a'ira Allah wa la ash-shahra al-haram wa la al-hady wa la al-qala'id wa la ammina al-bayta al-haram yabtaghuna fadlan min Rabbihim wa ridwana — Do not violate rites Allah — sha'a'ir — symbols — sacred months, hady sacrificial animals, qala'id garlands marking them, nor those heading Sacred House seeking bounty pleasure.\n\nWa idha halaltum fastadu — When exit ihram you may hunt.\n\nWa la yajrimannakum shana'anu qawmin an saddukum an al-masjidi al-haram an ta'tadu — Let not hatred of people who barred you from Sacred Mosque lead you transgress — as earlier Quraysh barred Hudaybiyyah, now not allow hatred to push injustice.\n\nWa ta'awanu alal-birri wat-taqwa wa la ta'awanu alal-ithmi wal-udwan wattaqu Allah — Help one another in birr and taqwa not sin transgression — birr defined earlier not faces east west but faith spending prayer etc (2:177) — taqwa best provision.\n\nInnAllaha shadidu al-iqab — Severe in punishment — as earlier severe punishment those who reject signs.\n\nRites, months, hady, qala'id — all sha'a'ir — symbols — respecting symbols part taqwa (2:158 Safa Marwah among symbols).\n\nHatred not justify transgression — justice even against those barred you — as earlier stand firm justice even against yourselves parents (4:124).\n\nNext forbidden foods listed.",
  "3": "Forbidden to you carrion, blood, swine flesh, what slaughtered for other than Allah, strangled, beaten, fallen, gored, eaten by wild beast except you slaughtered — what sacrificed on altars, divining with arrows — all that is wicked — Today disbelievers despaired of your religion — so do not fear them fear Me — Today I perfected your religion for you completed My favour and approved Islam as way — if forced by hunger not inclining to sin — Allah Forgiving, Merciful.\n\nHurrimat alaykum al-maytatu wad-damu wa lahmul-khinzir wa ma uhilla li-ghayri Allahi bihi wal-munkhaniqatu wal-mawqudhatu wal-mutaraddiyatu wan-natihata wa ma akala as-sabu'u illa ma dhakkaytum wa ma dhubiha ala an-nusub wa an tastaqsimu bil-azlam — Forbidden list: maytah carrion not slaughtered, blood flowing, swine flesh, dedicated to other than Allah — same as 2:173 four plus additional: strangled, beaten, fallen from height, gored, eaten by beast except if slaughtered before death — you slaughter quickly.\n\nWa ma dhubiha ala an-nusub — sacrificed on altars idols — and divining arrows — tastaqsimu bil-azlam — seeking fortune via arrows.\n\nDhalikum fisq — All that is wicked — fisq — stepping outside.\n\nAlyawma ya'isa alladhina kafaru min dinikum fa-la takhshawhum wakhshawni — Today disbelievers despaired of your religion so not fear them fear Me — ya'isa — despaired.\n\nAlyawma akmaltu lakum dinakum wa atmamtu alaykum ni'mati wa raditu lakum al-Islama dina — Today perfected your religion completed favour approved Islam as way — akmaltu — perfected, atmamtu — completed — This verse revealed at Arafah Day during Farewell Hajj.\n\nFa-man idturra fi makhmasatin ghayra mutajanifin li-ithmin fa-inna Allaha Ghafur Rahim — If forced hunger not inclining to sin — Allah Forgiving Merciful — necessity lifts prohibition as earlier (2:173) no sin if forced without wilful disobedience.\n\nPerfecting religion includes perfecting food laws, contracts.\n\nNext they ask what lawful.",
  "4": "They ask you what lawful for them — Say lawful are good things and what you trained of hunting animals and birds — you teach them from what Allah taught you — eat what they catch for you and mention Allah's name upon it — be mindful — Allah Swift in reckoning.\n\nYas'alunaka ma dha uhilla lahum quli uhilla lakum at-tayyibat wa ma allamtum min al-jawarih mukallibina tu'allimunahunna mimma allamaka Allahu fa-kulu mimma amsakna alaykum wadhkuru isma Allahi alayhi wattaqu Allaha... — Ask what lawful.\n\nUhilla tayyibat — good things — tayyib — as earlier eat from good things provided grateful (2:172) lawful and good — halal tayyib — not khabith.\n\nAnd what you trained of jawarih — hunting animals birds — mukallibina — training dogs etc — you teach them from what Allah taught you — as earlier He taught you what you did not know (4:104).\n\nEat what they catch for you and mention Allah name — wadhkuru isma Allah — as earlier mention Allah's name upon provision.\n\nWattaqu Allaha InnAllaha Sari'u al-hisab — Fear Allah Swift in reckoning — sari' al-hisab — as earlier Allah swift reckoning.\n\nTeaching hunting animals from what Allah taught — knowledge gift.\n\nMentioning Allah name upon hunting shows even hunting act worship.\n\nTayyibat good things — includes wholesome food, not filthy.\n\nNext today all good foods made lawful and food People Book lawful and women chaste.",
  "5": "Today all good foods made lawful for you — food of People Book lawful for you and your food lawful for them — and chaste women among believers and chaste women among those given Book before you when you give them dowries seeking chastity not fornication nor secret lovers — whoever disbelieves in faith his deed wasted Hereafter among losers.\n\nAlyawma uhilla lakum at-tayyibat wa ta'amu alladhina utu al-Kitaba hillun lakum wa ta'amukum hillun lahum... — Today tayyibat lawful — as previous verse tayyibat — food People Book lawful — ta'amu alladhina utu al-Kitaba — their slaughtered food if slaughtered properly with Allah name? Scholars differ but permitted.\n\nAnd chaste women — muhsanat — among believers and among People Book before you when give dowries seeking chastity not fornication nor secret lovers — muhsinin — as earlier seeking chastity not lust (4:23).\n\nWa man yakfur bil-iman fa-qad habita amaluh wa huwa fil-akhirati min al-khasirin — Who disbelieves in faith deed wasted Hereafter among losers — khasirin — losers as earlier losers.\n\nThis verse defines interfaith food and marriage: food People Book lawful, women chaste among People Book lawful for Muslim men (but not vice versa Muslim women to People Book men not lawful due to earlier polytheistic marriage prohibition and guarding faith of family, qiwamah). Chaste condition — muhsanah — chaste not fornicators.\n\nFaith condition: whoever disbelieves in faith — kufr after accepting — deed wasted.\n\nAfter perfecting religion (3) now details lawful.\n\nNext O you who believe when rise to prayer wash faces hands.",
  "6": "O you who believe, when you rise to prayer wash faces and hands to elbows and wipe heads and wash feet to ankles — if impure bathe — if ill or journey or came from privy or touched women find no water then dry ablution clean earth wipe faces hands — Allah wants not place difficulty but to purify you and complete favour so you may be grateful.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu idha qumtum ila as-salati faghsilu wujuhakum wa aydiyakum ila al-marafiq wamsahu bi-ru'usikum wa arjulakum ila al-ka'bayn — When rise to prayer wash faces hands to elbows wipe heads wash feet ankles — wudu steps.\n\nWa in kuntum junuban fattahharu — If impure bathe — ghusl.\n\nWa in kuntum marda aw ala safarin aw ja'a ahadukum min al-gha'it aw lamastum an-nisa fa-lam tajidu ma'an fa-tayammamu sa'idan tayyiban famsahu bi-wujuhikum wa aydikum minhu — Ill, journey, privy, touched women find no water then tayammum clean earth wipe faces hands — similar to 4:43 but now in Ma'idah after perfecting religion includes purification rites.\n\nMa yuridu Allahu li-yaj'ala alaykum min haraj wa lakin yuridu li-yutahhirakum wa li-yutimma ni'matahu alaykum la'allakum tashkurun — Allah not wants difficulty but to purify complete favour so you may be grateful — haraj — hardship — yutahhirakum — purify — yutimma ni'matahu — complete favour — la'allakum tashkurun — so grateful — as earlier Allah intends ease not hardship (2:185), wants lighten (4:27), not place difficulty but purify.\n\nTaharah — purification physical and spiritual — links wudu to inner purity earlier spend beloved, restrain anger etc.\n\nGrateful after purification.\n\nNext remember favour Allah and His covenant when you said we hear obey.",
  "7": "Remember favour of Allah upon you and His covenant when you said we hear and obey — fear Allah — Allah Knowing what is in chests.\n\nWadhkuru ni'mata Allahi alaykum wa mithaqahu alladhi wathaqakum bihi idh qultum sami'na wa ata'na wattaqu Allaha InnAllaha Alimun bi-dhati as-sudur — Remember favour Allah upon you and covenant He bound you when said sami'na wa ata'na — we hear and obey — same as believers say we hear obey (2:285, 3:53) and rasikhuna.\n\nWattaqu Allaha InnAllaha Alimun bi-dhati as-sudur — Fear Allah Knowing what chests contain — as earlier Knowledge what hearts hide.\n\nFavour includes perfecting religion completing favour approving Islam way (3), lawful tayyibat, food People Book, women chaste, wudu purification.\n\nMithaq — covenant — said we hear obey — at Hijrah? At Aqabah? When they pledged.\n\nKnowing what in chests — tests if truly hear obey or just say.\n\nNext O you who believe stand firm for Allah witnesses in justice.",
  "8": "O you who believe, stand firm for Allah as witnesses in justice — let not hatred of people cause you not be just — be just it is nearer to piety — fear Allah Allah Aware what you do.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu kunu qawwamina lillahi shuhada'a bil-qist wa la yajrimannakum shana'anu qawmin ala alla ta'dilu i'dilu huwa aqrabu lit-taqwa wattaqu Allaha InnAllaha Khabirun bima ta'malun — Stand firm for Allah witnesses justice — qawwamin lillah shuhada bil-qist — as earlier stand firm for justice witnesses even against yourselves parents (4:124) similar but here for Allah.\n\nWa la yajrimannakum shana'anu qawmin ala alla ta'dilu — Let not hatred people cause you not be just — shana'an — hatred — earlier same phrase do not let hatred of people who barred you from Sacred Mosque lead transgress help in birr taqwa (2).\n\nI'dilu huwa aqrabu lit-taqwa — Be just nearer to piety — taqwa.\n\nWattaqu Allaha InnAllaha Khabirun — Fear Allah Aware what you do — Khabir.\n\nJustice even against hated people — high standard.\n\nLinks to contracts fulfil, rites not violate.\n\nNext Allah promised those believed did righteous forgiveness great reward.",
  "9": "Allah promised those who believed and did righteous — forgiveness and great reward.\n\nWa'ada Allahu alladhina amanu wa amilu as-salihat lahum maghfiratun wa ajrun azim — Promise forgiveness and great reward — ajr azim — same as many earlier great reward after injury (3:177) and those who believed did righteous Gardens forgiveness (3:132).\n\nPromise includes after justice, purification.\n\nForgiveness first then great reward — as earlier forgiveness before Gardens.\n\nNext those who disbelieved denied signs companions Hellfire.",
  "10": "Those who disbelieved and denied Our signs — they are companions of Hellfire.\n\nWalladhina kafaru wa kadhdhabu bi-ayatina ula'ika Ashabu al-Jahim — Companions Hellfire — Ashab al-Jahim — companions Blaze.\n\nOpposite previous promise forgiveness great reward.\n\nKadhdhabu bi-ayatina — denied signs — signs include perfected religion, tayyibat lawful, wudu, etc.\n\nCompanions Hellfire abiding.\n\nNext remember favour when people intended stretch hands against you so He restrained their hands.",
  "11": "O you who believe, remember favour Allah upon you when people intended stretch hands against you so He restrained their hands from you — fear Allah — in Allah believers put trust.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu udhkuru ni'mata Allahi alaykum idh hamma qawmun an yabsutu ilaykum aydiyahum fa-kaffa aydiyahum ankum wattaqu Allaha wa ala Allahi falyatawakkali al-mu'minun — Remember favour when people intended stretch hands against you — yabsutu — extend — to harm — fa-kaffa — restrained hands — kaffa — withheld.\n\nWhen? At Badr? Or Jews Banu Nadir plotted kill Prophet dropping stone? Or conspiracy at Hudaybiyyah? Many occasions hand stretched then restrained.\n\nWattaqu Allaha wa ala Allahi falyatawakkali al-mu'minun — Fear Allah in Allah believers put trust — tawakkul — as earlier trust.\n\nFavour of protection — as earlier remember favour when intended stretch hands He restrained.\n\nAfter justice command, reminder Allah restrained hands of those who hated you — so be just even to them.\n\nNext Allah took covenant from Children Israel and We raised twelve leaders and said...",
  "12": "Allah took covenant from Children Israel and We raised among them twelve leaders and Allah said I am with you — if you establish prayer give zakah believe in My messengers support them and lend Allah good loan I will expiate your evil deeds and admit you Gardens beneath rivers — whoever disbelieves after that strayed from straight path.\n\nWa laqad akhadha Allahu mithaqa Bani Isra'ila wa ba'athna minhum ithnay ashara naqiban wa qala Allahu inni ma'akum la-in aqamtumu as-salata wa ataytumu as-zakata wa amantum birusuli wa azzartumuhum wa aqradtumu Allaha qardan hasanan la-ukaffiranna ankum sayyi'atikum wa la-udkhilannakum jannatin tajri min tahtiha al-anhar fa-man kafara ba'da dhalika minkum fa-qad dalla sawa'a as-sabil — Covenant Children Israel twelve naqib leaders — Allah said I with you — condition: establish prayer, give zakah, believe messengers support them, lend Allah good loan — qard hasan — as earlier who will lend good loan (2:245, 3:92, 5:12 etc) — expiate evil deeds admit Gardens.\n\nFa-man kafara ba'da dhalika fa-qad dalla sawa as-sabil — Who disbelieves after strayed straight path.\n\nCovenant details resemble believers: prayer zakah belief messengers support good loan.\n\nGood loan — qard hasan — spending from what love.\n\nIf fulfilled promise expiation Gardens — similar promise believers forgiveness great reward.\n\nIf disbelieves after — strayed.\n\nThis covenant history mirrors covenant with believers we hear obey (7).\n\nNext for breaking covenant We cursed them made hearts hard.",
  "13": "So for breaking their covenant We cursed them and made hearts hard — they distort words from places and forgot portion of what reminded — you will not cease to find betrayal from them except few — pardon them overlook — Allah loves doers of good.\n\nFa-bima naqdihim mithaqahum la'annahum wa ja'alna qulubahum qasiyah yuharrifuna al-kalima an mawadi'ihi wa nasu hazzan mimma dhukkiru bihi wa la tazalu tattali'u ala kha'inatin minhum illa qalilan minhum fa'fu anhum wasfah innAllaha yuhibbu al-muhsinin — Breaking covenant cursed, hearts hard — qasiyah — hard like stone (2:74 hearts hard).\n\nDistort words from places — yuharrifuna al-kalima — as earlier among Jews distort words (4:45) twisting tongues ra'ina, etc.\n\nForgot portion reminded — nasu hazzan — forgot share.\n\nYou will not cease find betrayal — kha'inah — betrayal from them except few — illa qalil.\n\nPardon overlook — fa'fu anhum wasfah — pardon them overlook — as earlier pardon overlook until Allah brings command (2:109) and pardon them seek forgiveness consult (3:159).\n\nAllah loves doers good — muhsinin.\n\nCurse hard hearts result breaking covenant — as earlier hearts sealed for disbelief (4:142) etc.\n\nDespite betrayal, pardon overlook command for Prophet? But also vigilance.\n\nLoves muhsinin — doers good even to those betraying.\n\nNext and from those who said we are Christians We took covenant but they forgot portion reminded so We stirred enmity hatred among them until Day.",
  "14": "And from those who said we are Christians We took covenant but they forgot portion of what reminded of — so We stirred among them enmity and hatred till Day — Allah will inform them of what they used to do.\n\nWa min alladhina qalu inna nasara akhadhna mithaqahum fa-nasu hazzan mimma dhukkiru bihi fa-aghrayna baynahum al-adawata wal-baghda'a ila yawmi al-qiyamati wa sawfa yunabbi'uhum Allahu bima kanu yasna'un — Christians covenant forgot portion — similar to Jews forgot.\n\nFa-aghrayna baynahum al-adawata wal-baghda — Stirred enmity hatred among them till Day — history Christian sects enmity.\n\nWill inform what they used to do — yunabbi' — on Day.\n\nForgetting portion reminded leads to enmity hatred among themselves — as earlier division after clear proofs (3:105) — forgetting covenant leads to division.\n\nEarlier rope holding together prevents division; forgetting causes division.\n\nNext O People Book Our Messenger has come making clear to you much of what you hid of Book and overlooking much.",
  "15": "O People of Book, Our Messenger has come to you making clear much of what you hid from Book and overlooking much — there has come to you from Allah light and clear Book.\n\nYa Ahla al-Kitabi qad ja'akum Rasuluna yubayyinu lakum kathiran mimma kuntum tukhfuna min al-Kitabi wa ya'fu an kathir qad ja'akum min Allahi nurun wa kitabun mubin — Messenger making clear much what you hid from Book — tukhfuna — hiding as earlier cast behind backs small price, mixing truth falsehood, twisting tongues.\n\nYa'fu an kathir — Overlooking much — not exposing all hidden to avoid embarrassment? Or pardoning?\n\nQad ja'akum min Allahi nurun wa kitabun mubin — Light and clear Book — nur mubin — as earlier nur mubin proof (4:157) — Quran light.\n\nLight makes hidden visible.\n\nMessenger clarifying hidden fulfills covenant making clear not hiding (3:187) — opposite of those who hid.\n\nFor People Book, light reveals what hid.\n\nGuide with it? Next.",
  "16": "With it Allah guides those who follow His pleasure to ways of peace and brings them from darkness into light by His permission and guides them to straight path.\n\nYahdi bihi Allahu man ittaba'a ridwanahu subula as-salam wa yukhrijuhum min az-zulumati ila an-nuri bi-idhnihi wa yahdihim ila siratin mustaqim — With it — light clear Book — Allah guides those follow His pleasure — ridwan — to ways peace — subul as-salam — plural ways peace — and brings them from darknesses into light by His permission — as earlier Allah Guardian believers brings darkness light (2:257, 3:???). And guides straight path.\n\nRidwan — pleasure — as earlier ridwan better than gardens (3:15), follow pleasure (5:2 seeking bounty pleasure).\n\nSubul as-salam — ways peace — salam — Islam, Paradise.\n\nDarknesses light — same as earlier.\n\nBy His permission — bi-idhnihi — as earlier bi-idhni Allah miracles.\n\nGuides straight path — sirat mustaqim — worship Allah my Lord your Lord (3:51).\n\nThis verse shows purpose light Book.\n\nNext disbelieved those who said Allah is Messiah son Maryam.",
  "17": "Certainly disbelieved those who said Allah is Messiah son Maryam — Say who can prevent Allah if He willed destroy Messiah son Maryam and his mother and all earth — to Allah belongs dominion heavens earth and what between — He creates what wills — Allah over all Capable.\n\nLaqad kafara alladhina qalu inna Allaha huwa al-Masihu ibnu Maryama qul fa-man yamliku min Allahi shay'an in arada an yuhlika al-Masiha ibna Maryama wa ummahu wa man fil-ardi jami'an wa lillahi mulku as-samawati wal-ard wa ma baynahuma yakhluqu ma yasha Wallahu ala kulli shay'in Qadir — Disbelieved those said Allah is Messiah son Maryam — direct deification.\n\nSay who can prevent Allah if He willed destroy Messiah son Maryam mother all earth — If Messiah god could not be destroyed, but Allah can destroy him — proves not god.\n\nTo Allah belongs dominion heavens earth and what between — mulk.\n\nYakhluqu ma yasha — Creates what wills — as earlier creates what wills (3:47 Isa creation).\n\nAllah over all Capable — Qadir — as Owner dominion gives whom wills.\n\nArgument simple: Messiah born, ate, mother ate, both would die if Allah willed destroy, so cannot be Allah who is Hayy Qayyum not overtaken drowsiness sleep.\n\nNext Jews Christians say we are children Allah beloved Say why then punish you for sins you are merely humans among those He created.",
  "18": "Jews and Christians say we are children of Allah and His beloved — Say why then does He punish you for sins? You are merely humans among those He created — He forgives whom wills punishes whom wills — to Allah belongs dominion heavens earth and what between — to Him destination.\n\nWa qalat al-Yahudu wan-Nasara nahnu abna'u Allahi wa ahibba'uh qul fa-lima yu'adhdhibukum bi-dhunubikum bal antum basharun mimman khalaq yaghfiru liman yasha wa yu'adhdhibu man yasha wa lillahi mulku as-samawati wal-ard wa ma baynahuma wa ilayhi al-masir — Claim children beloved — abna' Allah beloved — as earlier wishful thinking Fire few days, exclusive Paradise.\n\nQul fa-lima yu'adhdhibukum bi-dhunubikum — Why then punish you for sins? If children beloved unconditionally, why punish?\n\nBal antum basharun mimman khalaq — You merely humans among those He created — equal origin single soul (4:1).\n\nYaghfiru liman yasha wa yu'adhdhibu man yasha — Forgives whom wills punishes whom wills — same as near end Baqarah (2:284) forgives whom wills punishes.\n\nMulk heavens earth what between — and to Him destination — masir — same as to Allah destination (3:28 etc).\n\nChildren beloved claim refuted by punishment — if beloved unconditionally why punish? Real beloved forgiven? Actually beloved punished if disobey shows claim false.\n\nNext O People Book Our Messenger has come clarifying after gap between messengers lest you say no warner came.",
  "19": "O People of Book, Our Messenger has come to you clarifying after gap between messengers lest you say no bearer of good news nor warner came to us — bearer of good news and warner has come — Allah over all Capable.\n\nYa Ahla al-Kitabi qad ja'akum Rasuluna yubayyinu lakum ala fatratin min ar-rusul an taqulu ma ja'ana min bashirin wa la nadhir fa-qad ja'akum bashirun wa nadhir Wallahu ala kulli shay'in Qadir — Messenger clarifying after gap — fatrah — long period without messenger between Isa and Muhammad ~600 years — gap.\n\nLest you say no bashir nadhir came — excuse removed.\n\nBashir nadhir has come — same description Muhammad bearer good news warner (2:119) — same as prophets bearers good news warners so people no argument after (4:149).\n\nAllah over all Capable — Qadir same closing as previous — Capable send messenger after gap, capable destroy Messiah if willed, creates what wills.\n\nClarifying after gap shows mercy not leave without warner.\n\nNext remember Musa said O my people remember favour Allah upon you when He made among you prophets made you kings gave what not given to worlds.",
  "20": "And when Musa said to his people, O my people remember favour Allah upon you when He made among you prophets and made you kings and gave you what not given to worlds.\n\nWa idh qala Musa li-qawmihi ya qawmi udhkuru ni'mata Allahi alaykum idh ja'ala fikum anbiya'a wa ja'alakum mulukan wa atakum ma lam yu'ti ahadan min al-alamin — Musa says remember favour when made among you prophets — many prophets from Bani Israel — made you kings — mulukan — free, owning selves after Pharaoh slavery — gave what not given to worlds — sea split, manna, etc, preferred over worlds at time (2:47,2:122).\n\nUdhkuru ni'mata — Remember favour — as earlier O Children Israel remember favour (2:40).\n\nProphets among you, kings among you — leadership.\n\nGiven what not given worlds — shows bounty Owner great bounty.\n\nAfter gap between messengers, reminder Musa favour.\n\nNext O my people enter Holy Land which Allah decreed for you do not turn back.",
  "21": "Moses continues his impassioned address to the Israelites, building on the remembrance of divine favours. The command is direct and momentous: “Enter the Holy Land.” This refers to the land of Palestine, specifically Jerusalem and its surroundings, which had been promised to the descendants of Abraham.\n\nThe word “destined” in the translation captures the term *kataba*, which linguistically means “written. ” Allah had written this destiny for them, ordaining it as a place of settlement and worship, but this divine decree was conditional upon their active obedience and willingness to strive for it. The command therefore demolishes the notion of a passive reliance on destiny. It is not enough that God has written something good for a community; they must rise and walk into that promise with their own feet, facing its challenges.\n\nThe second part of the verse is a severe warning: “And do not turn back or else you will become losers. ” Turning back, in this context, refers to retreating from the battlefield of obedience out of fear of the enemy. It is a psychological and physical flight that reveals a deep failure of trust in God’s promise. The consequence of such a retreat is explicitly labelled as “losers.\n\n” This is not a loss of land or wealth, but a comprehensive state of failure in this world and the next. For the Muslim community in Medina, who were about to face major military tests and who would one day be called to spread the message, this verse was a direct parallel. They too had been given a divine mission and a covenant. The lesson was clear: the sacred mission cannot be abandoned at the first sight of a powerful adversary. When God has decreed a duty, the only path that avoids loss is to move forward with courage. Fear of the enemy's power betrays a heart that has forgotten the power of the One who made the promise. This verse thus serves as a timeless criterion for any generation of believers called to a difficult collective duty, reminding them that retreat from God’s command, when the path is clear, is the very definition of spiritual bankruptcy.",
  "22": "This verse records the immediate, disheartening response of the Israelites to Moses’ command, revealing the vast gulf between a prophetic leader’s trust in God and the masses’ trust in material power. Their reply begins with the respectful address “O Moses,” yet the content betrays a complete psychological defeat. They describe the inhabitants of the Holy Land as “an enormously powerful people.” The description fixes their gaze on the apparent strength of the enemy, a strength of body and military might that their own eyes magnified into an insurmountable obstacle. Their conclusion is absolute and cowardly: “so we will never be able to enter it.” They shut the door on the possibility of victory before a single step was taken, because their assessment was based purely on physical calculation.\n\nHowever, they add a condition that exposes their passive, conditional obedience: “until they leave.” This is a condition they know is unrealistic. God did not command them to wait for a miracle that required no effort; He commanded them to enter and fight. Their statement is effectively a refusal dressed up in pious-sounding language.\n\nThe phrase “If they do, then we will enter” is a mockery of faith. Faith does not wait for the path to be cleared by circumstances before it obeys; it steps forward trusting that God will clear the path. The psychological parallel for the early Muslims was powerful. They too faced “enormously powerful people” in the Quraysh and the Byzantine and Persian empires. The lesson from this narrative was a warning: if they respond to the Prophet’s call to struggle with the same materialistic analysis and conditional submission, they will share in the same humiliation. The verse serves as a permanent diagnosis of a diseased mindset in any believing community, a mindset that magnifies the strength of the opponent and minimises the power of the Lord, thereby paralysing all action and reducing faith to a mere theory that never touches the battlefield of life.",
  "23": "In the midst of the collective failure of nerve, a righteous minority steps forward to speak the language of courage and divine reliance. The verse introduces them as “Two God-fearing men—who had been blessed by Allah.” The description “God-fearing” highlights their deep consciousness of God, which prevented cowardice from taking root in their hearts.\n\nThe phrase “who had been blessed by Allah” indicates they possessed a special gift of insight, firmness, and wisdom. According to many commentators, these two men were Joshua and Caleb, the only two from that generation who maintained their faith and were later permitted to enter the Holy Land. Their strategic advice is brilliant in its simplicity: “Surprise them through the gate.” They acknowledge the strength of the enemy, but instead of being paralysed by it, they propose a tactical approach that relies on initiative and shock, not on waiting for the enemy to vacate the land. They do not deny the reality of the powerful inhabitants, but they see a way to victory that the fearful masses missed. Following the tactical advice, they make a strong promise grounded in faith: “If you do, you will certainly prevail.” This certainty is not based on military superiority but on the divine promise that God grants victory to those who obey Him. The key condition for this victory is then stated: “Put your trust in Allah if you are truly believers.” Trust, or tawakkul, is the active state of the heart that combines taking all worldly means with a complete reliance on God for the outcome. The statement “if you are truly believers” is a sharp rebuke. It ties the very authenticity of their claim to faith to their willingness to act and trust. This teaches that belief is not a static identity but a dynamic force that manifests as courage in the face of danger. For the Muslims, this was a direct lesson: the consultation of the righteous few who remind the community of God’s power and the necessity of trust must be heeded, and true iman is proven not in times of safety but in the moments that demand stepping through the gate.",
  "24": "Despite the powerful counsel of the two righteous men, the response of the Israelites moves from fearful refusal to an astonishing level of insolence and rebellion. The verse begins with “Yet,” indicating a stubborn persistence in their stance. They reiterate their refusal with even stronger emphasis: “O Moses! Still we will never enter as long as they remain there. ” They are now completely entrenched in their psychological paralysis, impervious to both spiritual motivation and sound strategic advice. But the true horror of this verse lies in what follows. They say, “So go—both you and your Lord—and fight; we are staying right here!\n\n” This statement crosses a line from cowardice into blasphemous disrespect. First, they speak of God and Moses as two separate entities who should go fight alongside each other, revealing a distorted and crude conception of the divine. Second, they absolve themselves of all responsibility, relegating the very prophet who led them out of slavery to the status of their mercenary, while they recline in comfort. Their declaration “we are staying right here” is a full embrace of a static, faithless life that refuses any cost. This verse captures the ultimate breakdown of the covenant community. When a people reach this stage of brazen defiance, they have not only lost their courage but have corrupted their understanding of God. The Quran immortalises this moment as a dire warning to the Muslim Ummah. The Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, was sent to call the believers to strive in God’s path. The response of the hypocrites and the weak of heart in Medina sometimes mirrored this attitude, with them offering excuses to avoid the heat of battle, leaving the Prophet and his closest companions to face the dangers alone. The verse serves as a painful mirror, asking every generation of believers if their response to the call of duty is one of active commitment or a shameful, seated refusal.",
  "25": "The profound grief and frustration of Moses reach their peak in this intimate prayer. Having exhausted his persuasive efforts and witnessed the public rebellion of his people, he turns entirely to the only source of help.\n\nThe word “pleaded” translates a supplication made in a state of deep distress. He confesses his human limitation with a tone of lament: “My Lord! I have no control over anyone except myself and my brother.” Moses, the mighty prophet who faced Pharaoh, acknowledges that his authority is powerless against hearts that have chosen rebellion. He can command, advise, and warn, but he cannot force belief or courage into a heart that has sealed itself shut.\n\nThe mention of “my brother” refers to Aaron, his co-prophet and supporter, who stood with him in this trial. By stating that he only has control over himself and his brother, Moses declares the limits of his responsibility. A prophet is not accountable for the sins of his people after he has delivered the message and done his utmost.\n\nThis is a profound comfort for every leader and caller to God who is met with stubborn rejection. Having established the limits of his control, Moses then asks for a divine judgment that separates: “So set us apart from the rebellious people. ” This is not a prayer born of hatred but of a desire for a clear divine verdict. He asks God to distinguish between the obedient minority and the defiant majority, both in destiny and in worldly affairs, so that justice can be seen. For the Prophet Muhammad, this verse was a direct consolation. He too would witness his followers waver and some hypocrites refuse the call. The lesson was that a prophet’s duty is to convey, strive, and then entrust the final judgment to God. The prayer of Moses becomes a model for the believer who has done all they can: turn to Allah, acknowledge human limits, and leave the fate of the rebellious to the All-Wise Judge.",
  "26": "The divine response to Moses’ plea is a decree of punishment that directly answers their own words. The rebellious Israelites had declared, “We are staying right here,” refusing to enter the Holy Land. So Allah decreed that they would remain in the wilderness, barred from their destination: “Then this land is forbidden to them for forty years.” The very thing they refused to fight for would be denied to them, and the state of wandering they chose out of cowardice would become their enforced reality. The forty-year period was not a random number; it was a precise duration during which an entire generation of slave-minded cowards would die out in the desert of Sinai, so that a new generation, born in freedom and hardened by the desert, would be the ones to enter under Joshua’s leadership. The description of their punishment continues: “during which they will wander through the land.” This wandering, or *tīh*, was a state of utter confusion and aimlessness. Despite the vastness of the desert, they would roam lost, unable to find a way out, a psychological torment that mirrored their spiritual lostness. Then Allah directly addresses the grieving prophet: “So do not grieve for the rebellious people.\n\n” This command is a balm for the heart of Moses. It is natural for a sincere prophet to grieve for his people’s fate, but God instructs him that such grief should not be wasted on those who have wilfully chosen rebellion and earned His wrath. This line is a crucial lesson in emotional and spiritual boundaries. The caller to truth can feel sadness but must not let it consume them, because the divine judgment is perfectly just and the rebels have brought their fate upon themselves.\n\nFor the Muslim community, the story ends with a clear moral: the consequence of refusing to struggle in God’s path, of prioritising safety over obedience, is not immediate annihilation but a prolonged, wasted existence of spiritual and existential wandering, a life barred from the true blessings and victories God had in store for them.",
  "27": "The narrative lens now shifts from the collective story of the Israelites to the very first human story of envy and murder, which serves as a timeless archetype of the human condition. The command “Relate to them in truth, O Prophet,” instructs Muhammad, peace be upon him, to recite this story to his people with absolute precision, for it contains essential wisdom for the community.\n\nThe story begins with Adam’s two sons, Abel and Cain, each offering a sacrifice. The core of the event is that “Abel’s offering was accepted while Cain’s was not. ” The Quran does not detail the sacrifices here, but authentic traditions explain that Abel, a shepherd, offered his finest, healthiest animal, while Cain, a farmer, offered some of his worst produce. Their hearts were different, and thus the divine acceptance was different. The reaction of Cain is immediate and violent: he “threatened, ‘I will kill you!’” The first murder in human history was not over wealth or territory but over religious envy—a sick heart that could not bear to see his brother favoured by God.\n\nThis is a powerful warning to all believers that a soul lacking sincerity may turn on those who are more pious, accusing them of hypocrisy or self-righteousness, when in reality the problem is a corrupt inner state. Abel’s response is a masterpiece of righteous composure. He says, “Allah only accepts the offering of the sincerely devout.” He identifies the root cause with clarity: his brother’s rejection was not a matter of ritual but a matter of the heart’s orientation.\n\nThe word *muttaqīn*, translated as “sincerely devout,” refers to those who possess taqwa, a God-consciousness that purifies intention. Abel’s reply is not a boast about his own piety; it is a theological explanation meant to direct his brother’s anger towards self-reform, not murder. The story thus teaches that the correct response to being surpassed in virtue is introspection, not aggression, and that the foundation of every accepted deed is the quality of taqwa residing in the heart.",
  "28": "This verse continues Abel’s dignified and principled response to his brother’s murderous threat, presenting one of the most powerful declarations of moral restraint in scripture. Abel is fully aware of the danger he faces; Cain’s hand is poised to strike.\n\nYet he states, “If you raise your hand to kill me, I will not raise mine to kill you.” This is not a statement of physical inability or cowardice. Abel is not saying he lacks the strength or the right to defend his life. Rather, he is making a conscious, ethical choice to not enter into a spiral of retaliation. He refuses to become a murderer even in the act of self-defence, because killing his brother would make him the same kind of transgressor. This sets an extraordinarily high moral standard, one that is not legislated as a general rule—Islamic law later permits self-defence—but is presented here as a personal, saintly virtue in a specific context. The reason for this choice follows immediately: “because I fear Allah—the Lord of all worlds.” The fear of Allah, this profound taqwa, completely overrides the instinct for vengeance. Abel’s vision of the world is not limited to this fleeting life. He sees beyond the immediate threat, connecting his action to the Lord of all worlds, the Master of every creation. His fear of God’s punishment and his hope for God’s pleasure are stronger than his fear of Cain’s hand. This teaches a profound lesson about the hierarchy of fears in a believer’s heart. When the fear of the Creator is truly alive, the fear of any created being shrinks to insignificance. Abel becomes the archetype of the peaceful believer, the one who absorbs violence rather than propagates it, whose faith is so deep that he entrusts his very life to divine justice. This spirit is reflected in numerous hadiths that praise the believer who controls anger and refrains from strife, even when he is in the right, leaving his ultimate vindication to God.",
  "29": "Abel’s speech reaches its conclusion with a statement that reveals not a passive submission to death, but a profound understanding of divine justice and an unexpected form of care for his own soul. He says, “I want to let you bear your sin against me along with your other sins.\n\n” The phrase “I want” shows that Abel is not merely resigned to his fate but actively wills it for a higher purpose. He does not desire his brother’s destruction out of hatred; he is laying out the stark reality of what Cain’s action will bring upon himself. By murdering him, Cain will not only carry the sin of the murder but will also add that to his “other sins,” meaning the earlier sins of envy, a rejected offering, and a corrupted heart. Abel is essentially saying, “My blood will be a burden around your neck on the Day of Judgment.” The result is then stated with clarity: “then you will be one of those destined to the Fire.” Abel is not making a judgment from his own ego; he is stating the divine law as revealed to him. The act of murder, especially of a righteous believer, is a sin that leads directly to Hellfire unless met with sincere repentance.\n\nThe final sentence, “And that is the reward of the wrongdoers,” universalises the principle. It is not just Cain who receives such a punishment; every wrongdoer who transgresses against others will face the same crushing justice. Abel’s words serve as a mirror for all tyrants and murderers. They may succeed in spilling blood in this world, but they are bankrupting their own Hereafter in a transaction of ultimate loss.\n\nFor the believer who reads this verse, it inspires a sombre reflection: the one who refrains from retaliation is not weak; he is ensuring that he stands on the Day of Judgment with a clean record, while his oppressor appears laden with the sins he committed. It is the ultimate victory of the victim, a victory that is not seen by the eyes of this world but is eternal in the next.",
  "30": "After the brilliant and truthful exhortation of Abel, the Quran records the tragic climax with a devastating brevity. The phrase “Yet Cain convinced himself” translates the Arabic *fa-ṭawwa‘at lahu nafsuhu*, which literally means his soul made the act easy and agreeable for him.\n\nThis is the terrifying anatomy of sin. The human soul, when left unchecked by taqwa, slowly rationalises the worst actions until they appear not only acceptable but desirable. Cain’s initial envy brewed into anger, his anger was not checked by his brother’s wisdom, and now his own inner whispers have decorated the heinous act of fratricide as a justified solution to his wounded pride. There is no external devil mentioned here; the focus is on the internal enemy, the nafs, which can become a greater enemy than any external shaytan.\n\nThe verse then states the grim action simply: “so he killed him.” The Quran does not linger on the gruesome details of the first murder. The starkness of the statement itself conveys the horror. In one moment of uncontrolled rage, the sacred bond of brotherhood was shattered, and a human soul, innocent of any crime, was sent to meet its Lord. Immediately, the divine verdict is pronounced: “becoming a loser.” The loss here is absolute and total. He lost his brother, his innocence, his peace of mind, his relationship with his Lord, and his Hereafter. In a worldly sense, he might have thought he had won by eliminating the source of his envy, but the Quranic reality flips this perception completely. He is the *khāsir*, the one in a state of utter bankruptcy.\n\nThis is the final destination of every person who lets their envy and anger dictate their actions. The verse serves as a timeless warning that the one who transgresses against another human being ultimately destroys nothing but their own soul. The loss in the divine ledger is total, even if the worldly ledger shows a temporary, blood-stained gain.",
  "31": "After the horrific act of murder, the narrative takes a darkly ironic and deeply instructive turn, detailing the immediate aftermath that compounds Cain’s misery. The verse states that “Allah sent a crow digging a grave in the ground.” The crow is an animal of lowly status, a scavenger. God inspired this crow to scratch at the earth to bury another dead crow. The purpose was “to show him how to bury the corpse of his brother.\n\n” This is a moment of profound humiliation for the human being who had been honoured with knowledge. Cain, a son of a prophet, had just committed the first murder but had absolutely no idea what to do with the body. He was carrying his brother’s corpse on his back, confused and haunted. The fact that he needed a bird to teach him the most basic act of covering the dead revealed the utter regression of his intelligence caused by his sin. Seeing the crow’s action, he cried out in anguish, “Alas! Have I even failed to be like this crow and bury the corpse of my brother?” This exclamation is a confession of his own inferiority in that moment. A creature of instinct displayed more practical wisdom than the human who had let his passion destroy his reason. He was not like the crow; he was lower, for the crow disposed of the dead with dignity while he had caused the death.\n\nThe verse ends with a note on his emotional state: “So he became regretful.” However, the scholars of tafsir note that this regret, or *nadamah*, was not the regret of sincere repentance that wipes out sin. It was the regret of a criminal who is sorry for the consequences of his crime, the inconvenience and the humiliation, not a remorse that turns back to God with a broken heart seeking forgiveness. There is no record of Cain repenting. His regret was the bitter fruit of guilt, a fire that burns in the heart but does not illuminate the path back to the Lord.",
  "32": "This verse transitions from the narrative of Cain and Abel to a grand universal principle, which is the direct legal and moral lesson derived from that first murder. The opening phrase “That is why” connects the decree directly to the story. Because a single act of envy led to the first unjust killing, Allah ordained a law that magnifies the weight of human life to an almost incomprehensible degree. The law was specifically written for the Children of Israel, but its principle is enshrined in Islamic ethics as well. The decree states that “whoever takes a life—unless as a punishment for murder or mischief in the land—it will be as if they killed all of humanity.” The unjust killing of one soul is equated with the murder of all people.\n\nThis is not a mere exaggeration; it is a profound ethical truth. The person who kills one innocent human being demonstrates a willingness to violate the sanctity of life itself. They have shredded the universal respect for human existence, and if left to their own devices, their disregard for life could extend to many. It is the principle of the sanctity of life in its most powerful expression. The only exceptions are clearly defined: lawful retribution for murder, and punishment for “mischief in the land,” which scholars interpret as crimes that destabilise society, such as terrorism and highway robbery. The opposite side of the coin is equally powerful: “whoever saves a life, it will be as if they saved all of humanity. ” Saving a life here refers to any act that prevents a wrongful death—rescuing someone from danger, advocating for a wrongly accused person, or even refraining from killing when one has the power to do so.\n\nThe verse then adds a sorrowful historical note: “Although Our messengers already came to them with clear proofs, many of them still transgressed afterwards through the land.” This indicates that even with this sacred law revealed, the Israelites, like many later communities, still violated it, shedding blood and causing corruption, proving that laws alone are not enough without hearts filled with taqwa.",
  "33": "This verse reveals the severe worldly punishment for a specific category of criminals who combine terrorism, apostasy, and banditry. The crime is defined as “waging war against Allah and His Messenger and spreading mischief in the land.” This refers to acts of armed aggression that terrorise a peaceful society, such as highway robbery, murder, and destabilising the state that implements divine law. The verse is often called the verse of *hirābah*. The prescribed punishments are listed, and scholars have extensively discussed their application based on the severity of the crime. The options are “death” for those who kill and terrorise, “crucifixion” after execution as a public deterrent for those who combine murder with robbery, “cutting off their hands and feet on opposite sides” for those who engage in armed robbery without taking a life, and “exile from the land” for those who cause terror but stop short of these acts. The range of penalties gives the Islamic judge discretion to match the punishment to the crime. The purpose is explicitly stated as a “disgrace for them in this world,” meaning a public humiliation that serves as a deterrent and an affirmation of the state’s authority to protect its citizens.\n\nHowever, the verse does not stop at the worldly punishment. It adds the terrifying spiritual consequence: “and they will suffer a tremendous punishment in the Hereafter.” This means that even if a criminal is executed, the worldly punishment does not expiate the sin before God, unless they repented before being apprehended, as the next verse clarifies. The severity of this law is a direct extension of the sanctity of life principle in the previous verse. Because human life is so valuable, those who shamelessly violate it and disrupt the peace of society face the sternest possible recompense in this life and the next, establishing a climate of safety for the common people.",
  "34": "Immediately after the harsh and detailed punishment for hirabah, the Quran opens a wide door of divine mercy, demonstrating that the law’s ultimate goal is not vengeance but the reformation of society and the individual. The condition for this escape from the worldly punishment is clear and time-bound: “those who repent before you seize them.” Repentance must occur before the criminal is captured by the authorities. If they turn themselves in, overcome by remorse and a genuine desire to reform, the prescribed worldly punishment for the crime of waging war against the society is dropped.\n\nThis is a powerful legal and spiritual incentive. It encourages the criminal to abandon their path of violence and surrender peacefully, knowing that they can find complete amnesty from the state’s penalty. This fosters a practical mechanism for reducing violence without a protracted manhunt.\n\nHowever, the phrase “before you seize them” is crucial. Once the criminal is captured, the door for amnesty from the worldly punishment closes, because at that point surrender is no longer a voluntary act of repentance but a consequence of being overpowered.\n\nThe verse then highlights the divine attributes that ground this leniency: “then know that Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The guarantee of forgiveness for the one who repents is absolute, because these two names of God envelop the sinner in a promise of pardon. This does not necessarily mean they are absolved from the rights of their victims; many scholars state that the victims’ rights, such as blood money, remain intact and must be fulfilled. But the state’s right to punish is waived.\n\nThe sequence of these two verses is a profound illustration of the balance between justice and mercy in Islamic law. The severity of the previous verse protects society, while the mercy of this verse protects the soul of the sinner who desires to change. It tells every human being that no matter how grave the crime, as long as the heart is still beating and the person is free to choose, the path back to God is open.",
  "35": "After the intense legal passages dealing with murder, terrorism, and theft, the Quran once again addresses the believers with a comprehensive call to spiritual and practical action, providing the formula for true success. The verse opens with the core command of the faith: “O believers! Be mindful of Allah.\n\n” This is the foundational taqwa, the constant awareness that acts as an internal moral compass. Immediately, a second command is added, which explains how to achieve and deepen that taqwa: “and seek what brings you closer to Him.\n\n” The word for “seek” is *ibtighā'*, implying active, persistent searching. The “what brings you closer” translates *al-wasīlah*, a term that encompasses every permissible means that elevates one’s rank before God. This includes obligatory acts of worship, abundant voluntary prayers, charity, seeking beneficial knowledge, maintaining family ties, and serving the creation. It is a call to be proactive in one’s spiritual life, not merely to avoid sin but to aggressively pursue the practices and stations that draw the divine love.\n\nThe third command forms a triad of personal piety, devotional pursuit, and societal struggle: “and struggle in His Way.” The term *jāhidū* is used, the root word of jihad, which encompasses every form of striving for God’s cause, from the internal struggle against one’s ego to the external struggle of defending the community and conveying the message. This struggle is the testing ground of the faith.\n\nThe verse ends with the goal of all these actions: “so you may be successful.” The word *tufliḥūn*, from *falāḥ*, denotes the ultimate, comprehensive success that is not just material prosperity but the eternal well-being of the soul.\n\nThe sequence is beautiful: taqwa is the inner light, seeking wasilah is the active devotional life, jihad is the external proof of sincerity, and falāḥ is the promised fruit of this complete programme. This verse gives the Muslim a concise roadmap for a life that leads to true victory.",
  "36": "The surah now shifts back to the fate of those who reject faith, painting a vivid and terrifying picture of the Day of Judgment that serves as a stark contrast to the \"success\" promised to the believers. It begins with \"As for the disbelievers,\" immediately defining the group whose destiny is about to be described. The hypothetical scenario presented is one of absolute material wealth: \"even if they were to possess everything in the world twice over.\n\n\" This is a rhetorical device that pushes the imagination to its limits. Imagine a person who owns not just the entire earth's riches—all the gold, oil, lands, and treasures—but double that amount. Such wealth, in worldly terms, would be sufficient to buy anything a human heart could desire.\n\nThe verse then places this unimaginable fortune in the context of the Afterlife: \"and offer it all to ransom themselves from the punishment of the Day of Judgment.\" In this world, a wealthy person can often buy their way out of legal trouble. The Quran shatters this illusion. On that Day, there is no currency, no negotiation, and no bail. The divine verdict is stated with crushing finality: \"it would never be accepted from them.\" The rejection is absolute. Their fate is sealed not by a lack of resources but by the nature of that Day, where the only acceptable currency is a sound heart, faith, and righteous deeds.\n\nThe verse concludes with the sentence: \"And they will suffer a painful punishment.\" The word \"painful,\" translating *alīm*, indicates a pain that permeates the very core of one's being, a suffering that is both physical and spiritual. This verse is meant to shake the conscience of the listener. It warns the believers against being impressed by the material wealth of those who reject God, for on the Day of Judgment, that wealth will be less than worthless. It also instills a deep gratitude for the promise of forgiveness given to the believers, a gift that all the treasures of the earth could never purchase.",
  "37": "This verse adds further terrifying details to the fate of the disbelievers, moving from the Day of Judgment’s courtroom to the actual experience within Hell. The psychological state of the damned is captured in the phrase \"They will be desperate to get out of the Fire.\" The desperation is an intense, panicked craving, a desire for escape that overwhelms every other thought. When a person is trapped in a worldly fire, every fibre of their being screams to flee the flames. This verse magnifies that instinct to an eternal scale. They will wish for death, they will wish for oblivion, they will wish for any exit.\n\nHowever, the divine will is unalterable: \"but they will never be able to.\" Their powerlessness is absolute. There is no eventual release, no parole, no annihilation of the self. Their wish is perpetually denied, adding a profound psychological torment to the physical pain.\n\nThe verse then concludes with the definitive statement: \"And they will suffer an everlasting punishment.\" The word \"everlasting\" translates *muqīm*, meaning established, permanent, and continuous without interruption. This affirms the Islamic doctrine that Hell is an eternal abode for those who die upon disbelief.\n\nThis teaching is not a matter of divine cruelty but of divine justice, as they had been given a lifetime of opportunities, clear signs, and warnings, yet they persistently rejected the truth and would have remained in that state had they lived forever. The sequence of verses 36 and 37 serves a clear function in the surah. After detailing the punishments for worldly crimes like hirabah and theft, the Quran reminds the listener that these punishments, however severe, are finite and can even be nullified by repentance. The punishment of the disbelievers, however, is a stark reminder that the ultimate catastrophe is not a worldly penalty but the eternal loss of the Hereafter.\n\nThis contrast is designed to reorder the believer’s priorities and to instill a healthy fear that propels one toward the success mentioned in verse 35.",
  "38": "The surah returns to the realm of criminal law, having established the grand ethical principles about the sanctity of life and the need for societal peace. This verse addresses the crime of theft, a violation of property rights that erodes economic trust and security. It begins by explicitly mentioning both genders: \"As for male and female thieves.\n\n\" This is a profound statement of legal equality, ensuring that a woman's punishment for theft is the same as a man's, unlike the pre-Islamic customs that often treated women with harsher or more lenient standards arbitrarily. The prescribed punishment is stated with stark clarity: \"cut off their hands.\n\n\" The Arabic word *yad* refers to the hand, and the juristic consensus is that for a first-time offence meeting all strict conditions, the right hand is amputated from the wrist. The condition for this punishment is that the theft must involve a minimum value of stolen goods, taken from a secure location, without any plausible claim of ownership or need-driven justification.\n\nThis is not a punishment for a hungry person stealing food out of necessity, which is excused by the law of necessity. The rationale is immediately stated: \"for what they have done—a deterrent from Allah.\n\n\" The word \"deterrent,\" *nakālan*, implies an exemplary punishment that serves to prevent others from committing the same crime. It is a measure that protects the property of the entire community by making the cost of theft so severe.\n\nHowever, the verse closes with two divine names that frame the entire ruling: \"And Allah is Almighty, All-Wise. \" The name \"Almighty\" indicates that He has the authority to enact such a law, and no human sentimentality can override it. The name \"All-Wise\" is crucial; it affirms that this punishment, which may seem harsh to modern sensibilities that prioritise the criminal's comfort over the victim's security, is rooted in a perfect wisdom that understands human nature, the need for a safe society, and the ultimate balance of justice and mercy. The wisdom is seen in the high standards of evidence required, the detailed conditions for amputation, and the provision for repentance in the next verse.",
  "39": "Following the same merciful pattern seen after the punishment for hirabah, this verse opens the door of divine forgiveness for the thief who reforms, though it does not explicitly cancel the worldly punishment if the crime has already been proven and the punishment is due. The verse states, \"But whoever repents after their wrongdoing and mends their ways.\" True repentance here is defined by two elements: remorse for the past sin and a concrete, visible amendment of behaviour. The thief must not only feel sorry but must also return the stolen property to its rightful owner if possible, and change their lifestyle to one of honesty and integrity. This act of genuine, transformative repentance triggers a magnificent divine promise: \"Allah will surely turn to them in forgiveness.\n\n\" The term *yatūbu*, from *tawbah*, means that Allah Himself turns towards the servant with His mercy and pardon. It is a beautiful image of a reciprocal relationship: the servant turns away from sin, and the Lord turns towards the sinner with forgiveness. The certainty of this promise is emphasised with the word \"surely. \" Then the verse concludes with the deeply comforting pair of divine names: \"Indeed, Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\n\" The word \"All-Forgiving\" (*Ghafūr*) means He covers the sin and wipes its record clean, while \"Most Merciful\" (*Raḥīm*) means He bestows blessings and love upon the repentant soul. This verse, when read with the previous one, perfectly illustrates the Islamic balance. The law on earth serves to protect society and deter crime through a visible, severe punishment. The law of the Hereafter serves to redeem the individual soul that genuinely changes. No matter how grave the sin—whether terrorism, murder, or theft—the penitent heart that turns to its Creator before death finds an ocean of mercy. This prevents the criminal from falling into despair, assuring them that their past does not permanently define them as long as they perform a sincere tawbah that mends their relationship with God and with the creation they have wronged.",
  "40": "This verse serves as a majestic conclusion to the extended legal discourse, bringing the reader’s attention back to the absolute sovereignty and authority of the Lawgiver. It begins with a rhetorical question: \"Do you not know that the kingdom of the heavens and the earth belongs to Allah alone?\n\n\" This is a gentle rebuke aimed at awakening the innate knowledge that every soul possesses about God’s absolute ownership. Everything in the universe, from the vast galaxies to the cells in a thief's hand, belongs to Him. The laws He decrees are not the impositions of an outsider but the rulings of the true Sovereign over His own dominion. This realization is meant to foster a deep submission to the rulings that preceded it. Why should a human being object to the punishment for theft or for waging war, when the One who commanded it owns the very hand being cut and the life being taken?\n\nThe verse then declares a principle that governs the operation of this divine kingdom: \"He punishes whoever He wills and forgives whoever He wills. \" This statement is not a license for arbitrary will devoid of wisdom, as the names Al-Hakīm (All-Wise) and Al-‘Adl (The Just) are fundamental. Rather, it means that no one can force Allah’s hand. His punishment and forgiveness are not subject to external approval. He punishes those who meet the conditions for His punishment according to His perfect knowledge, and He forgives those who meet the conditions for His mercy. It silences the human arrogance that questions why certain sins are met with certain penalties, reminding the believer that the criteria belong to the All-Knowing.\n\nThe verse ends with the comprehensive affirmation: \"And Allah is Most Capable of everything.\" His capability encompasses the enforcement of His laws, the resurrection of the dead for judgment, and the execution of His perfect will in both worlds. This final note is a warning and a comfort: a warning to those who think they can evade the divine law, and a comfort to those who trust that the Almighty is fully able to grant them the success and forgiveness He promised in the earlier verses.",
  "41": "O my people, enter Holy Land which Allah decreed for you and do not turn back on heels otherwise you will be losers.\n\nYa qawmi udkhulu al-arda al-muqaddasata allati kataba Allahu lakum wa la tartaddu ala adbārikum fa-tanqalibu khasirin — O my people enter Holy Land — muqaddasah — purified — which Allah kataba — decreed, written for you — do not turn back heels — ala adbarikum — otherwise losers — khasirin — as earlier losers who trade belief.\n\nArd muqaddasah — blessed land — Sham? Bayt al-Maqdis — purification.\n\nKataba Allahu lakum — decreed for you — if you obey enter.\n\nDo not turn back — irtidad — turning back as earlier turn back after belief (3:86 etc).\n\nKhasirin — losers — same as losers.\n\nHistorical: Bani Israel feared inhabitants giants.\n\nNext they said O Musa in it are mighty people we will not enter until they leave — if they leave we will enter.",
  "42": "They said, O Musa in it are mighty people and we will never enter as long as they remain — go you and your Lord and fight, we will sit here.\n\nQalu ya Musa inna fiha qawman jabbarina wa inna lan nadkhulaha hatta yakhruju minha fa-in yakhruju minha fa-inna dakhilun — In it mighty people jabbarin — giants — we will never enter as long remain — lan nadkhulaha — refusal.\n\nQalu ya Musa idhhab anta wa Rabbuka fa-qatila inna ha-huna qa'idun — Go you and Lord fight, we will sit here — qa'idun — sitters as earlier qa'iduna not equal mujahidun (4:90) — lagging (4:69).\n\nContrast with hawariyyun said we helpers Allah (3:52) and companions Muhammad said if you and Lord fight we with you.\n\nThis shows cowardice after favour prophets kings what not given worlds (20).\n\nNext Musa said My Lord I control only myself and brother so separate us from defiantly disobedient.",
  "43": "He said, My Lord I control only myself and brother — so separate us from defiantly disobedient people.\n\nQala Rabbi inni la amliku illa nafsi wa akhi fa-fruq baynana wa bayna al-qawmi al-fasiqin — I control only self and brother — Harun.\n\nFa-fruq — separate — between us and fasiqin — defiantly disobedient.\n\nAfter people said go you and Lord fight we sit, Musa despairs control.\n\nLa amliku illa nafsi — control only self — responsibility individual, after arranging (4:67 take precautions go forth groups) etc.\n\nFasiqin — defiantly disobedient same as those who turn away after covenant (3:82) etc.\n\nNext He said then it is forbidden for them forty years wandering earth — do not grieve over defiantly disobedient.",
  "44": "He said, It is forbidden for them forty years — wandering in earth — do not grieve over defiantly disobedient.\n\nQala fa-innaha muharramatun alayhim arba'ina sanah yatihuna fil-ard fa-la ta'sa ala al-qawmi al-fasiqin — Forbidden for them forty years wandering — tih — lost in Sinai desert.\n\nMuharramah — forbidden entry Holy Land due disobedience.\n\nForty years — generation replacement — those jabbarin fearful die, new generation courageous enter with Yusha ibn Nun.\n\nLa ta'sa — do not grieve — same as do not weaken grieve you uppermost if believers (3:135) — do not grieve over fasiqin.\n\nWandering tih symbolizes loss of guidance despite having prophet among them — similar to those who purchased error wandering blindly (2:15 prolong transgression).\n\nNext recite to them story two sons of Adam truthfully when they offered sacrifice accepted from one not other.",
  "45": "Recite to them story of Adam's two sons in truth — when they offered sacrifice — accepted from one not other — he said I will surely kill you — other said Allah only accepts from mindful.\n\nWatlu alayhim naba'a ibnay Adam bil-haqq idh qarraba qurbanan fa-tuqubbila min ahadihima wa lam yutaqabbal min al-akhar qala la-aqtulannaka qala innama yataqabbalu Allahu min al-muttaqin — Recite story two sons Adam — Habil and Qabil? — in truth — bil-haqq — true.\n\nOffered sacrifice — qurban — accepted from one not other.\n\nSaid I will kill you — threat envy as earlier envy people bounty (4:53) many among People Book wish turn you back envy (2:109).\n\nOther said Allah only accepts from mindful — muttaqin — same as best provision taqwa, haqqa tuqatih, etc.\n\nTaqwa condition acceptance — not mere ritual. Same as Ibrahim raised foundations said accept from us — taqabbal minna (2:127).\n\nLesson: envy leads murder first murder history; acceptance criteria taqwa not lineage.\n\nNext if you stretch hand to kill me I will not stretch mine to kill you I fear Allah Lord worlds.",
  "46": "If you stretch hand to kill me I will not stretch mine to kill you — I fear Allah Lord of worlds.\n\nLa-in basatta ilayya yadaka li-taqtulani ma ana bi-basitin yadiya ilayka li-aqtulak inni akhafu Allaha Rabba al-alamin — If stretch hand kill me I not stretch mine kill you — I fear Allah.\n\nNon-aggression despite threat.\n\nFear Allah Lord worlds — same fear as should be feared (3:101).\n\nThis statement of Habil shows non-violence principle: not initiate.\n\nFear Allah prevents retaliation murder.\n\nContrast with earlier if you fear breach send arbitrator desire reconciliation (4:34) — seeking peace.\n\nIkhafu Allaha Rabba al-alamin — earlier wife Imran dedicated, Musa etc fear.\n\nNext I want you to bear my sin and your sin so be among companions Fire — that recompense wrongdoers.",
  "47": "Indeed I want you to bear my sin and your sin so you be among companions Fire — that recompense of wrongdoers.\n\nInni uridu an tabu'a bi-ithmi wa ithmika fa-takuna min Ashabi an-Nar wa dhalika jaza'u az-zalimin — I want you bear my sin and your sin — i.e., sin of killing me plus your previous sins — so be companions Fire — recompense wrongdoers.\n\nTabu'a bi-ithmi — bear burden — not transferring automatically but cause.\n\nThis shows consequence murderer bears both.\n\nJaza'u az-zalimin — recompense wrongdoers — same as recompense disbelievers.\n\nNext soul prompted him to kill brother so killed him became among losers.",
  "48": "So his soul prompted him to kill his brother — he killed him and became among losers.\n\nFa-tawwa'at lahu nafsuhu qatla akhihi fa-qatalahu fa-asbaha min al-khasirin — Soul prompted — tawwa'at — made easy, decorated — nafs — same as those follow desires want deviate great deviation (26) and beautified desires (3:14).\n\nFa-qatalahu fa-asbaha min al-khasirin — Killed became among losers — khasirin — same as losers earlier who turned back heels (41) and who trade.\n\nFirst murder — envy prompted, soul made easy.\n\nBecame among losers despite thought killing would relieve envy.\n\nNext Allah sent crow searching ground to show how hide corpse brother ashamed — said woe to me unable be like crow hide corpse — became among regretful.",
  "49": "Then Allah sent a crow searching in ground to show him how to hide corpse of brother — He said Woe to me unable to be like this crow to hide corpse brother — became among regretful.\n\nFa-ba'atha Allahu ghuraban yabhathu fil-ardi li-yuriyahu kayfa yuwari saw'ata akhih qala ya waylata a'ajaztu an akuna mithla hadha al-ghurabi fa-uwariya saw'ata akhi fa-asbaha min an-nadimin — Allah sent crow — ghurab — yabhathu — searching ground — to show how hide — yuwari saw'ata — corpse — saw'ah — shame? Body after death considered saw'ah to be hidden respectfully.\n\nQala ya waylata — Woe to me unable like crow — became among nadimin — regretful — regret after murder not repentance? Regret worldly exposure? Or regret sin?\n\nCrow teaches burial — first burial lesson via animal — humility human learns from animal.\n\nFrom single soul created many (4:1) now first death burial.\n\nBecame among nadimin — regretful — not necessarily forgiven but regret.\n\nNext for that We decreed for Children Israel whoever kills person except for soul or corruption earth as if killed all humanity whoever saves it as if saved all humanity — messengers came clear proofs then many after that transgress earth.",
  "50": "Because of that We decreed for Children Israel — whoever kills a person not for a soul nor corruption in earth — as if killed all humanity — and whoever saves one as if saved all humanity — Our messengers came to them with clear proofs then many after that transgress earth.\n\nMin ajli dhalika katabna ala Bani Isra'ila annahu man qatala nafsan bi-ghayri nafsin aw fasadin fil-ardi fa-ka-annama qatala an-nasa jami'an wa man ahyaha fa-ka-annama ahya an-nasa jami'an — Because of that first murder We decreed Bani Israel law murder consequence — whoever kills one person not for murder retribution or corruption earth — spreading mischief — as if killed all humanity — because sanctity one equals all, as from single soul (1) spreading many.\n\nWhoever saves one — ahyaha — gives life, pardons, protects — as if saved all.\n\nThis deen life preservation — life in retribution (2:179) — there is life.\n\nMessengers came clear proofs then many after transgress earth — despite decree.\n\nThis verse foundation for sanctity life, used today but often quoted without condition — exception for soul (qisas) or fasad fil-ard (spreading corruption) — not absolute pacifism but preserved life except just cause.\n\nSaving one as if saved all humanity shows value each soul.\n\nNext recompense those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive corruption earth.",
  "51": "Indeed recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger and strive to spread corruption in earth is they be killed or crucified or hands feet cut opposite or exiled from land — that disgrace for them in world and in Hereafter great punishment — except those who repent before you overpower them — Allah Forgiving, Merciful.\n\nInnama jaza'u alladhina yuharibuna Allaha wa Rasulahu wa yas'awna fil-ardi fasadan an yuqattalu aw yusallabu aw tuqatta'a aydihim wa arjuluhum min khilafin aw yunfaw min al-ard dhalika lahum khizyun fid-dunya wa lahum fil-akhirati adhabun azim illa alladhina tabu min qabli an taqdira alayhim fa'lamu anna Allaha Ghafur Rahim — Recompense those wage war against Allah Messenger strive corruption — yuharibuna — harb — war — cutting roads, terror, armed robbery, terrorism — fasad — corruption.\n\nPunishment four options: killed, crucified, hands feet cut opposite, exiled — judge chooses matching crime severity — if killed and robbed, killed crucified; if killed only, killed; if robbed not killed, hands feet cut opposite; if terrorized without killing robbing, exile.\n\nKhizyun fid-dunya humiliation world great punishment hereafter.\n\nExcept those repent before overpowered — tawba before capture — Allah Forgiving Merciful — exception shows door repentance even for harabah.\n\nThis law protects society from terror, preserves life earlier.\n\nConnects to previous whoever kills one as if killed all — these wage war kill many, punishment severe.\n\nNext O you who believe fear Allah seek means to Him strive path so you may succeed.",
  "52": "O you who believe, fear Allah, seek means to Him and strive in His path so you may be successful.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu ittaqu Allaha wabtaghu ilayhi al-wasila wa jahidu fi sabilihi la'allakum tuflihun — Fear Allah, seek wasila — means — to Him — closeness — and strive path so succeed — tuflihun — falah as earlier farmer success.\n\nWasilah — means to approach — good deeds: prayer, zakah, spending beloved, remembering standing sitting, holding fast rope, etc.\n\nJahidu fi sabilihi — strive path — as earlier fight for weak, fight allies Satan plot weak, strive wealth selves.\n\nSuccess — falah — earlier best nation successful (3:104), rope, etc.\n\nAfter severe punishment harabah, way to success is taqwa, wasilah, jihad path.\n\nWasilah includes what Allah taught you from hunting (4), good things lawful (5), etc.\n\nNext those disbelieved if had all earth and like to ransom from punishment Day not accepted.",
  "53": "Indeed those who disbelieved — if they had all what is in earth and like with it to ransom themselves from punishment Day — not accepted — painful punishment.\n\nInna alladhina kafaru law anna lahum ma fil-ardi jami'an wa mithlahu ma'ahu li-yaftadu bihi min adhabi yawmi al-qiyamati ma tuqubbila minhum wa lahum adhabun alim — If had all earth and like with it to ransom — fidya — from punishment Day not accepted — as earlier earth full gold ransom not accepted (3:90) and ransom not accepted (2:48).\n\nPainful punishment.\n\nEarth full gold cannot purchase iman after death.\n\nNext they will want exit Fire but not exit — lasting punishment.",
  "54": "They will want to exit Fire but will not exit it — lasting punishment.\n\nYuriduna an yakhruju min an-nar wa ma hum bi-kharijina minha wa lahum adhabun muqim — Want exit Fire but not exit — muqim — lasting — as earlier abiding therein.\n\nAfter ransom not accepted, desire exit not granted.\n\nContrast believers Gardens rivers eternal.\n\nNext male female thief cut hands recompense example deterrent from Allah — Allah Mighty, Wise.",
  "55": "Male thief and female thief — cut their hands as recompense for what they earned — deterrent from Allah — Allah Mighty, Wise.\n\nWassariqu wassariqatu faqta'u aydiyahuma jaza'an bima kasaba nakalan min Allah Wallahu Azizun Hakim — Thief male female — sariq — cut hands — yad — as recompense earned — nakal — deterrent example.\n\nAziz Hakim — Mighty Wise — as earlier Mighty Wise shaping wombs, etc.\n\nCutting hand seems severe but preserves property, deters as earlier harsh punishment for harabah, qisas life.\n\nNakal — deterrent — prevents.\n\nConditions for cutting strict: minimum value, from secure place, not need, etc — scholars detailed.\n\nAfter ransom not accepted and desire exit not exit, now theft punishment in world.\n\nNext whoever repents after wrongdoing and corrects — Allah will turn to him — Allah Forgiving, Merciful.",
  "56": "Whoever repents after his wrongdoing and corrects — Allah will turn to him — Allah Forgiving, Merciful.\n\nFa-man taba min ba'di zulmihi wa aslahā fa-inna Allaha yatubu alayh InnAllaha Ghafur Rahim — Repents after wrongdoing — zulm — and corrects — aslahā — Allah will turn — yatubu — accept.\n\nGhfur Rahim — Forgiving Merciful — as after theft, ransom, Fire.\n\nRepentance includes returning stolen property? Scholars: repentance for theft requires returning.\n\nAslahā — correct — not only feel regret but correct — return, fix.\n\nAcceptance door still for thief, harabist before overpowered.\n\nNext do you not know to Allah belongs dominion heavens earth He punishes whom wills forgives whom wills — Allah over all Capable.",
  "57": "Do you not know to Allah belongs dominion heavens earth — He punishes whom He wills and forgives whom He wills — Allah over all Capable.\n\nAlam ta'lam anna Allaha lahu mulku as-samawati wal-ard yu'adhdhibu man yasha wa yaghfiru liman yasha Wallahu ala kulli shay'in Qadir — Do you not know — alam ta'lam — to Allah belongs dominion heavens earth — mulk — as Owner dominion gives whom wills (3:26), punishes whom wills forgives whom wills — same as near end Baqarah (2:284) forgives punish, and (3:129) forgives punish.\n\nAllah over all Capable — Qadir.\n\nDominion includes right to legislate punishments cutting hand, harabah, etc, and right forgive whom wills if repent correct.\n\nAfter repentance forgiveness, reminder dominion.\n\nNext O Messenger do not grieve those who rush to disbelief among those said we believe with mouths hearts not believed and among Jews listeners to lies listeners to people not come to you distort words.",
  "58": "O Messenger, do not grieve over those who rush to disbelief among those who said we believe with mouths but hearts not believed and among Jews listeners to lies — listeners to people who did not come to you — distort words from places saying if you are given this take it if not beware — those Allah does not want to purify hearts — for them disgrace in world great punishment Hereafter.\n\nYa ayyuha ar-Rasul la yahzunka alladhina yusari'una fil-kufri min alladhina qalu amanna bi-afwahihim wa lam tu'min qulubuhum wa min alladhina hadu samma'una lil-kadhib samma'una li-qawmin akharina lam ya'tuka yuharrifuna al-kalima min ba'di mawadi'ihi yaquluna in utitum hadha fa-khudhuhu wa in lam tu'tawhu fahdharu wa man yuridillahu fitnatahu fa-lan tamlika lahu min Allahi shay'an ula'ika alladhina lam yuridi Allahu an yutahhira qulubahum lahum fid-dunya khizyun wa lahum fil-akhirati adhabun azim — Do not grieve those rush disbelief among those said belief mouths not hearts — hypocrites — and among Jews listeners lies listeners to people not come — double spying.\n\nDistort words from places after places saying if given this take if not beware — twist as earlier distort words from places (4:45).\n\nThose Allah does not want purify hearts — fitnah — test.\n\nDisgrace world great punishment hereafter.\n\nMessenger grieving over rushers disbelief as earlier do not grieve rushers disbelief (3:176) — same.\n\nListening to lies — samma'una lil-kadhib — eager to hear lies.\n\nNext listeners to lies devourers of forbidden — if come judge between them or turn away.",
  "59": "Listeners to lies, devourers of forbidden — if they come to you judge between them or turn away — if turn away not harm — if judge judge with justice — Allah loves just.\n\nSamma'una lil-kadhib akkaluna lis-suht fa-in ja'uka fahkum baynahum aw a'rid anhum wa in tu'rid anhum fa-lan yadurruka shay'an wa in hakamta fahkum baynahum bil-qist InnAllaha yuhibbu al-muqsitin — Listeners lies devourers forbidden — suht — haram — bribes, riba, etc.\n\nIf come to you — People Book coming to Prophet to judge — judge between or turn away — option.\n\nIf turn away not harm — no harm.\n\nIf judge with justice — qist — Allah loves just — muqsitin — as earlier judge with justice (4:57) and Allah loves doers good, loves just.\n\nSuht — forbidden — includes bribery judges — as earlier do not consume wealth unjustly nor bring to judges to consume portion sinfully while know (2:188).\n\nNext how can they make you judge while with them Torah containing judgment Allah then turn away after — those not believers.",
  "60": "How can they make you judge while with them Torah containing judgment Allah then they turn away after — those not believers.\n\nWa kayfa yuhakkimunaka wa indahum at-Tawratu fiha hukmu Allah thumma yatawallawna min ba'di dhalika wa ma ula'ika bil-mu'minin — How make you judge while Torah with them containing judgment Allah then turn away after — if Torah contains stoning adulterer, they hide?\n\nWa ma ula'ika bil-mu'minin — Those not believers — because turn away after judgment against desire.\n\nThis shows seeking taghut judgment earlier — want taghut when convenient, want Prophet when convenient pick.\n\nTrue believers make you judge without discomfort (4:64) submit fully. These make you judge then turn away after — not believers.\n\nNext indeed We sent down Torah containing guidance and light by which prophets who submitted judged.",
  "61": "This verse continues the detailed portrait of the hypocrites from among the Jewish tribes in Medina, exposing the profound duplicity that characterised their interaction with the Muslim community. The phrase “When they come to you, believers” describes their physical approach to the gatherings of the Prophet and his companions. Upon entering this sacred space, their tongues utter the words, “We believe.\n\n” This is a verbal claim of faith, a public declaration meant to secure the social and political benefits of being considered part of the Muslim ummah. However, the verse immediately shatters this outward profession by revealing their inner state: “But they are committed to disbelief when they enter and when they leave.\n\n” The Arabic construction here, using the word *dakhalū*, indicates that they are already immersed in a state of kufr when they arrive, and they remain in that same state when they depart. The encounter with the believers and the hearing of the Quran changes nothing in their hearts. They came as disbelievers and they leave as disbelievers, their outer confession being nothing more than a theatrical performance.\n\nThis is a terrifying diagnosis of a heart that has become immune to the truth; it can sit in the company of the Prophet, hear the words of God, and still walk away completely unmoved because its inner commitment to rejection is absolute. The verse then seals this exposure with the statement, “And Allah knows what they hide.\n\n” This is both a comfort to the believers, who might be deceived by the hypocrites’ apparent friendliness, and a dire warning to the hypocrites themselves. They may conceal their mockery, their secret alliances with the enemies of Islam, and their true theological positions, but the One before whom all secrets are laid bare is fully aware. This knowledge is not passive; it is the prelude to a just reckoning. The lesson for all believers is the immense value of sincerity, ikhlas. A person can be physically present in the most blessed gatherings, yet gain nothing because the heart is absent or, worse, hostile. The verse prompts a regular self-examination: when I enter the company of the faithful or the house of God, and when I leave, has my state of faith been increased, or have I merely performed a social ritual while my heart remained locked in its own world of disobedience and doubt?",
  "62": "After exposing the hidden disbelief of the hypocrites, this verse turns to their visible, outward behaviour, which provides the evidence of their inner corruption. The Prophet is addressed, and through him every observer: “You see many of them racing towards sin, transgression, and consumption of forbidden gain.” The verb “racing” is highly significant. It indicates not a reluctant fall into error, but an eager, competitive haste to commit wrongdoing. They are not merely sinners; they are athletes of sin, striving to outdo one another in wickedness. The three categories of their vice are then listed. “Sin” here is *ithm*, the general category of any deed that violates divine law and harms the soul. “Transgression” is *‘udwān*, the act of overstepping limits, of being aggressive and unjust towards others, a direct violation of the command to cooperate in goodness and not in sin.\n\nThe third and most specific is “consumption of forbidden gain,” translating *suht*, which, as explained earlier in the surah, refers primarily to bribery, usury, and any wealth acquired through utterly corrupt means, such as taking money to alter a judgment. This greed was a hallmark of the leadership that had lost all fear of God.\n\nThe verse ends with the divine verdict: “Evil indeed are their actions!” This is not a human judgment but God’s own assessment of their deeds. It teaches the believer to develop a moral clarity that is not swayed by the apparent worldliness or sophistication of such people. Their cleverness, their wealth amassed through suht, and their political manoeuvring are all, in the final analysis, nothing but evil.\n\nThe verse also serves as a warning about the social environment. When a community sees its members racing towards sin and no one stops them, it is a sign of deep sickness. It calls the believers to be a people who race towards forgiveness and good deeds, as described elsewhere in the Quran, rather than towards the things that earn divine condemnation. The visible conduct of a person or a group is a reliable indicator of the state of their heart, and the actions of these hypocrites, a frantic scramble for sinful profit and bribery, revealed the emptiness of their claim to faith.",
  "63": "The Quran now turns its scrutiny from the sinful masses to the religious leadership, exposing a failure that is even more catastrophic than the sins themselves. The question is a sharp rebuke: “Why do their rabbis and scholars not forbid them?” The rabbis, *rabbāniyyūn*, and the scholars, *aḥbār*, were the custodians of the Torah, the spiritual guides entrusted with teaching and correcting the community. They possessed the knowledge of what God had prohibited. They could see their people racing towards sinful speech, such as mocking the Prophet and distorting scripture, and towards suht, the consumption of bribery and usury.\n\nYet they remained silent. The verse demands an answer for this silence.\n\nThis is a foundational principle in Islam: the obligation of enjoining good and forbidding evil, *al-amr bil-ma‘rūf wa-n-nahy ‘ani-l-munkar*, is not merely an individual virtue but a collective responsibility. When those with knowledge and authority fail to speak out against clear violations of divine law, they become complicit in the sin. Their silence is not neutral; it is a form of approval that allows evil to spread and corrupt the entire society.\n\nThe verse then pronounces a separate condemnation specifically on their inaction: “Evil indeed is their inaction!” The Arabic word used is *mā kānū yaṣna‘ūn*, which refers to their habitual practice, their “crafting” of deeds. Their inaction is their main product, their defining work, and it is declared to be evil. A hadith of the Prophet, peace be upon him, powerfully illustrates this principle: “When people see an oppressor and do not restrain him, it is likely that Allah will punish them all with a widespread punishment.” The failure of the scholars is often the first step towards the downfall of a nation. This verse is a direct address to every Muslim scholar, teacher, and community leader. It is a warning that knowledge is a trust, and the one who sees a sin being committed openly and does not raise a voice of correction out of fear, laziness, or a desire to maintain worldly status, has betrayed that trust. The silence of the learned is the funeral prayer of a righteous society.",
  "64": "This verse records an appalling statement of blasphemy uttered by some among the Jewish community and delivers a multi-layered divine response. The historical context, as reported by commentators, is that some Jewish leaders, when the verse “Who will lend Allah a good loan?” was revealed, mockingly said, “Allah is poor and we are rich,” or, as this verse states, “Allah is tight-fisted,” implying that God withholds provision from them, and thus they cannot be expected to spend in charity. The immediate divine reaction is a curse: “May their fists be tied and they be condemned for what they said.” The punishment mirrors the sin; because they attributed stinginess to the Most Generous, their own hands are symbolically tied in miserliness and they are distanced from God’s mercy.\n\nThe verse then corrects the blasphemy with the beautiful truth: “Rather, He is open-handed, giving freely as He pleases.” The Arabic term *mabsūṭatān*, translated as “open-handed,” literally means “spread out,” a vivid image of unrestricted, abundant giving without limit. Allah’s generosity is not constrained by any economy; He gives to the believer and the disbeliever, the righteous and the sinner, in this world as a manifestation of His name Ar-Razzāq.\n\nThe verse then delivers a tragic prophecy about the effect of the Quran on such people: “That which has been revealed to you from your Lord will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief. ” The same revelation that heals and guides the sincere heart becomes a poison that increases the disease in a jealous, arrogant heart. Next, the verse proclaims a divinely imposed social curse: “We have stirred among them hostility and hatred until the Day of Judgment.” Because they rejected the unifying truth, they were left to the chaos of their own tribal and sectarian divisions, a state of perpetual internal conflict.\n\nThe verse then gives a powerful assurance of divine protection against their military schemes: “Whenever they kindle the fire of war, Allah puts it out. ” Every plot and alliance against the Prophet was foiled by God’s intervention. The final note condemns their ultimate activity: “they strive to spread corruption in the land. And Allah does not like corruptors.” The verse thus offers a complete cycle of their sin, punishment, God’s nature, the paradoxical effect of truth, and the ultimate failure of their conspiracies.",
  "65": "This short but profoundly merciful verse opens a window of hope immediately after the severe condemnations of blasphemy and corruption. It presents a conditional statement that reveals the heart of divine mercy and the true purpose of God’s dealings with humanity. The condition is placed on “the People of the Book,” the Jews and Christians, the very groups whose leadership had just been described with such dark imagery. They are not eternally condemned by race; their fate is tied to their response. The condition is that they become “faithful and mindful of Allah.\n\n” The word “faithful,” *āmanū*, means to embrace the full faith, which after the coming of the Prophet Muhammad necessarily includes believing in him and the Quran. The phrase “mindful of Allah” translates *ittaqaw*, meaning they would live with a constant, active consciousness of God, avoiding His prohibitions and fulfilling His commands. Had they done this, two magnificent divine acts would have followed. First, “We would have certainly absolved them of their sins.\n\n” The word *kaffarnā* means to cover, blot out, and expiate completely. Every sin from their past, including the blasphemies and the distortions, would have been erased as if they had never existed.\n\nThis is the tremendous promise of forgiveness that awaits every sincere repentant, regardless of the depth of their prior transgression. Second, “and admitted them into the Gardens of Bliss.” The Gardens are not merely ordinary paradises; they are *Jannāt an-Na‘īm*, the Gardens of pure, untainted delight and ease, the highest levels of eternal pleasure where every desire is fulfilled and every sorrow is absent. This verse thus demonstrates that God’s door is never shut. The very people whose beliefs and actions have been scrutinised and condemned are simultaneously being invited to a complete transformation that would change their eternal destiny from the Fire to the highest Bliss. It is a profound lesson in balancing fear with hope: the warnings are severe because the danger is real, but the invitation remains open because the Creator is, above all, the All-Forgiving and Most Merciful. No past is too stained to be purified by a sincere return.",
  "66": "This verse extends the conditional promise of the previous one, moving from the spiritual reward of forgiveness and Paradise to the tangible, worldly consequences of true adherence to divine guidance. The condition now is specifically about the scriptures: “had they observed the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to them from their Lord.” The “what has been revealed to them” is understood as the Quran, which is addressed to all humanity and is the final fulfillment of the scriptural promise. True observance is not merely a ritualistic reading but a comprehensive implementation of the guidance contained in these books, which would naturally lead the sincere follower to recognise and accept the final message. The result of this observance is described in breathtaking terms: “they would have been overwhelmed with provisions from above and below.\n\n” The phrase “from above” refers to the blessings of the sky: abundant rain in its proper seasons, spiritual illumination, and the descent of divine mercy. “From below” refers to the blessings of the earth: fertile soil, abundant crops, mineral wealth, and general economic prosperity. This teaches a direct correlation between a society’s collective righteousness and its material well-being. When a people live by God’s law, the natural world, which is also subject to God’s command, cooperates with them.\n\nThis is not a “prosperity gospel” in the simplistic sense, but a divine principle repeatedly affirmed in the Quran. However, the verse immediately balances this ideal picture with a realistic assessment of the present reality: “Some among them are upright.\n\n” This is a crucial recognition that generalisation is not absolute. Among the People of the Book, there was a minority that genuinely sought truth, who were just and humble, like Abdullah ibn Salam and the Negus of Abyssinia. But then the verse concludes with the sad reality: “yet many do nothing but evil.” The majority, due to their rejection and corruption, had made evil their only occupation.\n\nThis verse teaches the Muslim community the vital link between obedience and provision, and it also commands fairness in judgment, acknowledging the righteous minority while not being naive about the corrupted state of the majority.",
  "67": "This verse is a direct, powerful mandate to the Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, and through him to every caller to truth until the end of time. The address “O Messenger” singles him out for the weightiest of tasks. The command is absolute: “Convey everything revealed to you from your Lord.\n\n” The Arabic term *balligh* means to deliver completely, without omission, distortion, or concealment. The Prophet is not allowed to hide any part of the revelation, even if that part might provoke the anger, mockery, or opposition of the powerful. Some reports suggest that the Prophet, aware of the intense hostility from the Jews and hypocrites as detailed in the preceding verses, might have felt a natural human hesitation to deliver certain rulings that would be particularly difficult for them to hear, such as the exposure of their scriptural distortions or the condemnation of their theological errors. This verse orders him to suppress that hesitation entirely. The severity of the obligation is then emphasised with a striking statement: “If you do not, then you have not delivered His message. ” This does not suggest that the Prophet ever failed in his duty, but it underscores the principle that the messenger’s entire mission hinges on complete, fearless proclamation. A partial delivery is a nullification of the prophethood itself. Immediately following this command, a divine guarantee of protection is given: “Allah will certainly protect you from the people.” This was a specific promise to the Prophet that he would not be assassinated before his mission was complete. After this verse was revealed, it is reported that the Prophet dismissed his personal guards, saying, “Leave me, for my Lord has protected me.” This assurance is for every dai‘i: the one who delivers the truth faithfully is under a divine shield, and no harm can befall them except what Allah permits for a greater good.\n\nThe verse concludes, “Indeed, Allah does not guide the people who disbelieve,” meaning that the Prophet’s job is to convey, not to convert. The hearts that remain in disbelief despite the clear message are beyond his responsibility; their guidance is a matter for God alone.",
  "68": "This verse is a direct challenge to the People of the Book, stripping away their false sense of religious security. The Prophet is instructed to declare to them plainly, “You have nothing to stand on.\n\n” The phrase “nothing to stand on” translates *lastum ‘alā shay’*, meaning they have no valid religious foundation, no true faith, and no acceptable argument before God. This is a sweeping negation of their claim to be the chosen people of God based merely on their lineage or their lip service to their scriptures. The condition for having a legitimate standing is then stated: “unless you observe the Torah, the Gospel, and what has been revealed to you from your Lord.” True observance of the Torah and Gospel inherently entails believing in the prophecies within them that foretell the coming of a final Messenger and a final scripture. To claim adherence to Moses and Jesus while rejecting Muhammad is a self-contradiction, because Moses and Jesus, in their authentic teachings, commanded the worship of one God and foretold the seal of the prophets.\n\nThus, their very salvation, according to their own books, depends on their acceptance of the Quran. The verse then revisits the tragic reality of their response: “And your Lord’s revelation to you, O Prophet, will only cause many of them to increase in wickedness and disbelief.” The very truth that should have been their salvation becomes a means of their spiritual destruction because they meet it with envy and arrogance instead of humility. As in verse 64, this is a law of spiritual psychology: the same sun that softens wax hardens clay.\n\nFinally, the Prophet is consoled, “So do not grieve for the people who disbelieve. ” This is a direct echo of earlier consolations in the surah. The Prophet’s heart was heavy with the desire for their guidance. The command “do not grieve” is a liberation from an emotional burden that is not his to carry. He has delivered the message, established the clear proof, and the consequences of rejection are now between them and their Creator.\n\nThis is a lesson for every believer who feels sorrow over the misguidance of loved ones: the grief must not become a paralysing despair, and the responsibility ends where the clear delivery of truth ends.",
  "69": "This is one of the most famous yet often misunderstood verses of the Quran, and it must be understood within its clear context in Al-Ma'idah. The verse lists four categories: “the believers,” meaning the Muslims; “Jews”; “Sabians,” a group often described as monotheistic followers of a prophetic tradition prior to Islam; and “Christians.” The key to unlocking the verse is the conditional clause that follows: “whoever truly believes in Allah and the Last Day and does good.” The verse does not say that anyone who calls themselves a Jew or a Christian is automatically saved. It says that among all these groups, those individuals who possess true, authentic faith in God and the Last Day, combined with righteous deeds, are the ones who will be saved. Before the coming of the Prophet Muhammad, a Jew who sincerely followed the uncorrupted Torah, or a Christian who sincerely followed the authentic Gospel that preached pure monotheism, would be on the path of salvation.\n\nHowever, after the advent of the final Prophet, the very definition of “truly believes in Allah” must include belief in all His messengers, including Muhammad, peace be upon him. A person today who is aware of the Prophet’s message and rejects him cannot be described as having true faith in God, because they have rejected the very messenger and book that God sent to guide them. The great companion Ibn Abbas clarified that when Surah Al-Baqarah’s similar verse was revealed, it was later explained and contextualised by verses like “Whoever seeks a religion other than Islam, it will never be accepted from him.” This verse, therefore, is not a charter for religious pluralism where all paths are equally valid. It is a profound statement of divine justice: God judges individuals according to their sincere response to the truth that reached them. The reward for such sincere believers, from any age, is complete peace: “no fear for them, nor will they grieve. ” They will be spared the terror of the Day of Judgment and the sorrow of regret. The verse is a call to all humanity that the essential formula for salvation is unwavering monotheism, eschatological accountability, and virtuous action, a formula that is completely and perfectly embodied in Islam.",
  "70": "After the universal statement of salvation’s conditions, the Quran returns to the historical narrative of the Children of Israel to provide a concrete example of a people who broke the covenant and failed the test of responding to divine truth. The verse begins with the reminder, “Indeed, We took a covenant from the Children of Israel and sent them messengers.” This covenant was the solemn pledge to worship God alone, obey His law, and follow the prophets He would send. God, in His mercy, did not leave them with just a book; He continuously sent a stream of messengers to revive their faith, clarify their law, and warn them against deviation.\n\nHowever, the response to these divine favours is tragic: “Whenever a messenger came to them with what they did not desire, they denied some and killed others.” The phrase “with what they did not desire” is the psychological key to their rebellion. The prophets did not come to affirm their whims; they came with the truth, which often clashed with their material interests, their pride, and their deeply ingrained habits. When the message contradicted their desires, instead of subjugating their egos to the truth, they judged the truth by their egos. The result was a bifurcated atrocity. “They denied some” refers to those prophets they rejected as liars, refusing to accept their prophethood. “And killed others” is the chilling escalation. They murdered prophets like Zechariah and John the Baptist, and they attempted to kill Jesus.\n\nThis is the ultimate crime against divine guidance. The verse is a warning to the Muslim community not to repeat this pattern. The Quran and the Sunnah will also bring teachings that challenge the desires of the lower self. The true believer is the one who submits when the revelation comes, even if it is difficult, while the loser follows the path of the Israelites, accepting only what suits their convenience and rejecting, or even violently opposing, what does not. It is a lesson that the criterion of truth is not its alignment with human desire, but its divine origin.",
  "71": "This verse delves deeper into the psychology behind the Israelites' persistent rebellion, explaining the false sense of security that emboldened them. The phrase “They thought there would be no consequences” translates a very powerful Arabic expression that suggests they calculated that their crimes—denying and murdering prophets—would not result in a divine trial or punishment, a *fitnah*. They believed they were immune, as the supposed chosen people, or they thought God was too merciful to hold them severely to account.\n\nThis is the spiritual disease of *amnu makr Allāh*, feeling safe from the plan of God. This false hope led directly to their hardened attitude: “so they turned a blind eye and a deaf ear.\n\n” This is a metaphorical description of their wilful refusal to see the clear signs of the prophets and to hear the divine words they brought. It was not a physical disability but a chosen spiritual blindness and deafness. They had shut down the very faculties that could guide them to salvation. Despite this profound rebellion, the verse reveals a cycle of divine mercy: “Yet Allah turned to them in forgiveness after their repentance.” Every time they sank to a new low, if a sincere repentance emerged, Allah, in His boundless forbearance, accepted it and forgave them. This shows that the door of tawbah is never closed as long as the sun rises from its east.\n\nYet, the tragedy continues: “but again many became blind and deaf. ” After being forgiven and restored, the majority relapsed into their old ways, taking divine mercy for granted and returning to their arrogance. The verse concludes with the ever-present divine surveillance: “And Allah is All-Seeing of what they do.\n\n” This is a warning that the cycle of sin and relapse is fully documented, and while forgiveness is available, a final, decisive reckoning awaits. The lesson for the believer is to never feel secure from divine consequences, to constantly renew one’s repentance, and to avoid the hardened state where the eyes and ears no longer function for the truth.",
  "72": "The surah circles back to the core theological deviation of Christianity, having earlier dealt with their broken covenant and historical errors. The pronouncement is definitive: “Those who say, ‘Allah is the Messiah, son of Mary,’ have certainly fallen into disbelief.\n\n” This is an unambiguous declaration that the deification of Jesus is a negation of Islam’s fundamental truth. The verse immediately provides the internal evidence from the life of Jesus himself to refute this claim: “The Messiah himself said, ‘O Children of Israel! Worship Allah—my Lord and your Lord.’” This is a direct quote of Jesus’ core message as recorded in the Gospels, where he consistently directed worship to the Father. If Jesus worshipped God, called God his Lord, and instructed others to do the same, he cannot logically be that same God. The argument is simple and irrefutable.\n\nThe verse then states the universal law that applies to all who commit this act: “Whoever associates others with Allah in worship will surely be forbidden Paradise by Allah. ” The word for “associates others” is *yushrik*, the act of shirk, which is the only sin God explicitly states He will not forgive if a person dies upon it without repentance. The consequence is absolute exclusion from Paradise. The home of such a person is “the Fire,” an eternal abode.\n\nThe final clause, “And the wrongdoers will have no helpers,” strips away any hope of an intercessor who could overturn this decree. On the Day of Judgment, no saint, no angel, and not even Jesus himself will be able to help those who worshipped him instead of God.\n\nThis is a verse that draws the clearest possible red line. It does not merely state that the Christian theology is incorrect; it states that it expels a person from the boundaries of faith. The verse serves as a powerful call to pure monotheism, reminding all believers that the greatness of any prophet, even one as magnificent as the Messiah, should never lead to an elevation of his status to that of the Creator.",
  "73": "This verse addresses the second major Christian doctrine, the Trinity, which attempts to reconcile the divinity of Jesus and the Holy Spirit with a form of monotheism. The phrase “Allah is one in a Trinity” translates *thālithu thalāthah*, literally “the third of three.” This refers to the concept of three co-equal persons in one Godhead: the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit. The Quran declares that those who espouse this formula have “certainly fallen into disbelief,” just like those who simply say Jesus is God. The reason follows immediately: “There is only One God.\n\n” This is the bedrock of Islamic theology, *tawḥīd*, a singularity that is indivisible, uncompounded, and absolute. The idea of a plural Godhead, however philosophically explained, is a contradiction to the divine nature.\n\nThe verse then delivers a dire warning: “If they do not stop saying this, those who disbelieve among them will be afflicted with a painful punishment. ” The condition “if they do not stop” contains both a threat and a hidden invitation. The very fact that they are warned to “stop” implies that the door to repentance is still open. If they cease this belief and return to the pure monotheism that Jesus preached, they can escape the punishment. The punishment promised is “painful,” *alīm*, a torment that is intense and pervasive. This punishment can be understood as both worldly chastisement and the eternal punishment of the Hereafter. This verse, combined with the previous one, confronts the two primary theological pillars of historical Christian orthodoxy. It demonstrates that the Islamic critique is not a superficial polemic but a direct engagement with specific, defined doctrines. The lesson for the Muslim is the absolute importance of preserving the purity of tawḥīd in one’s belief and language, always resisting any subtle blurring of the line between the Creator and the most honoured of His creations.",
  "74": "In the immediate wake of the most severe condemnations of shirk, the Quran’s tone shifts into a tender, pleading invitation. The rhetorical question, “Will they not turn to Allah in repentance and seek His forgiveness?” is an expression of divine longing for the return of the sinners.\n\nThe word “turn” is *yatūbūn*, the same root as tawbah, indicating a complete turning back, a change in direction. The verse does not say, “Will they not become Muslims,” but focuses on the internal act of repentance, which is the heart of Islam. This teaches that the core of conversion is not just a change of label but a profound inner revolution where the soul turns from the worship of created beings to the worship of the Creator alone. The invitation to “seek His forgiveness” is a promise of a clean slate. Even the sin of declaring God to be a trinity or a man, a sin that fills the heavens and the earth, can be completely erased by a sincere seeking of divine pardon.\n\nThe verse then anchors this invitation in two of God’s most beautiful names: “And Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. ” The name Al-Ghafūr is in the intensive form, meaning He forgives again and again, covering vast, repeated sins. The name Ar-Raḥīm means His mercy is perpetual and flowing, enveloping the repentant sinner with love and care, not just a cold legal pardon. This verse is a masterpiece of da‘wah. It follows the Quranic method of using the threat of punishment to break the ego’s resistance, and then immediately applying the balm of mercy to attract the now-softened heart. It shows that no human being, no matter how deeply entrenched in false theology, is beyond the reach of divine forgiveness if they take the step of sincere repentance.\n\nFor the Muslim reader, it cultivates a hopeful outlook towards all people, reminding them that the greatest sinners of yesterday can become the greatest saints of tomorrow through the miracle of tawbah.",
  "75": "This verse lays out the Islamic doctrine of Jesus in its most straightforward, demythologised form, using pure logic to dismantle any claim to his divinity. The statement “The Messiah, son of Mary, was no more than a messenger” is the positive declaration of his true status. The emphatic phrase “no more than” is a rebuke to the two extremes: the Jews who rejected him as a false prophet, and the Christians who elevated him to God. The truth is the middle path. To prove his non-divinity, the verse provides a simple historical observation: “Many messengers had come and gone before him.” Jesus was not a unique phenomenon in the chain of prophethood. Prophets like Abraham, Moses, David, and John the Baptist came, delivered their message, and died. Jesus is part of this same human procession. His mother, Mary, is then described as “a woman of truth,” *ṣiddīqah*, a title of immense honour indicating her complete sincerity and affirmation of God’s word, but crucially affirming her full humanity. Then comes the final, devastatingly simple logical argument: “They both ate food.” Eating is the most basic biological function of a creature that is dependent on sustenance for survival. God, the Self-Subsisting, needs no food and experiences no hunger. If Jesus was God, he would not have been subject to the needs and limitations of a physical body. This single, observable fact of his human life shatters the logical foundation of his divinity.\n\nThe verse concludes with an expression of astonishment at the human capacity for self-deception: “See how We make the signs clear to them, yet see how they are deluded from the truth!” The signs are made manifest and clear, like a brilliant sun in the sky, yet the human mind, clouded by tradition, emotion, and blind following, can be turned away from such an obvious truth. It is a lesson in epistemology: clear evidence is not enough for a heart that does not wish to see.",
  "76": "This verse instructs the Prophet to confront the idolaters and the Christians with a rational argument that appeals to the most basic human logic regarding worship. The question “How can you worship besides Allah those who can neither harm nor benefit you?” is a challenge to the very purpose of worship. Worship, at its core, is an act of seeking benefit and protection from harm. A rational person directs their worship, their ultimate hope and fear, only towards a being that possesses the power to grant benefit and avert harm. The idols worshipped by the pagans, and the human beings like Jesus and Mary worshipped by the Christians, in their own earthly existence could not control their own destiny. Jesus could not prevent the plot of the Jews who sought to crucify him, nor could he provide sustenance for himself without God’s will. So why would a human being devote their ultimate devotion to another creature who is, in terms of independent power, completely impotent?\n\nThe second half of the verse provides the positive alternative, highlighting the attributes that make God alone worthy of worship: “And Allah alone is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. ” The name “All-Hearing” means He hears every prayer, every cry for help, every whispered secret, no matter the language or the number of simultaneous supplicants. The idols and the saints in their graves hear nothing.\n\nThe name “All-Knowing” means He is fully aware of every need, every pain, and every intention, even before it is expressed. The one who truly hears and the one who truly knows is the one who can truly respond. This verse is a masterclass in Islamic theology, reducing the entire debate to a simple criterion of power and knowledge. It teaches the believer to examine the object of their ultimate reliance. If it cannot independently hear, know, benefit, or harm, it is irrational to worship it. The only rational choice is the One who possesses the totality of these attributes in absolute perfection.",
  "77": "This verse is a direct prohibition of *ghuluww*, religious extremism and exaggeration, which is identified as the root cause of the theological deviations of the People of the Book. The call “O People of the Book” is an address to both Jews and Christians. The command “Do not go to extremes in your faith beyond the truth” encapsulates the Islamic position as the balanced middle way. For the Christians, the extreme was to raise a human prophet, Jesus, to the status of God. For the Jews, their extreme was the opposite: an excessive rejection and denigration of Jesus and a rigid, hypocritical legalism that killed the spirit of the law. The truth lies in between: honouring all prophets without deifying any of them.\n\nThe verse then traces the origin of these extremes: “nor follow the vain desires of those who went astray before you.” This refers to the early innovators and heretics who introduced these extreme ideas into the pure monotheistic tradition. The doctrine of the Trinity and the excessive veneration of saints and icons were not the teachings of Jesus but the products of later generations influenced by Greek philosophy and pagan culture. To follow these ideas is not to follow divine revelation but to chase the vain desires of misguided ancestors.\n\nThe final part of the verse describes the devastating impact of their straying: “They misled many and strayed from the Right Way.” The error of the leaders was not a private matter; it caused the mass misguidance of countless followers, a responsibility they will carry. The verse is a timeless warning for Muslims as well. The danger of *ghuluww* is a constant threat. Extremism in venerating the Prophet Muhammad or awliya’ to the point of attributing divine knowledge or power to them is the same error that led the Christians away from pure monotheism. The straight path is the path of moderation, love without deification, respect without worship.",
  "78": "This verse provides a powerful testimony that the condemnation of the rebellious Israelites is not unique to the Quran but is a consistent theme across the chain of scriptures. It states that the disbelievers among the Children of Israel “were condemned” on the tongues of two of their most revered prophets: David and Jesus, the son of Mary. David, to whom the Psalms, the Zabur, were revealed, pronounced divine curses upon the lawbreakers among his people. The Psalms are filled with imprecations against the wicked who oppress the poor and forsake God’s law. Jesus, the son of Mary, likewise pronounced woes and condemnations upon the hypocritical scribes and Pharisees who had corrupted the religion and rejected the truth he brought. The New Testament contains his severe rebukes, calling them a “brood of vipers” and foretelling the destruction of those who rejected the prophets. The Quran here appeals to the very scriptures the Jews and Christians hold sacred to prove that their own prophets testified against their rebellious behaviour. The cause of this prophetic condemnation is then stated: “That was for their disobedience and violations. ” Their transgression was of two kinds. First, *‘iṣyān*, a general state of disobedience to God’s commands, a lifestyle of sin. Second, *‘udwān*, which is specific and aggressive violation, overstepping the bounds and committing injustices against fellow human beings. The two terms together cover the vertical breach with God and the horizontal breach with humanity. This verse serves as a powerful lesson in accountability. It tells the believers that the narrative of the Quran about the Children of Israel is not a biased account but a truth that aligns with the witness of the prophets who lived among them. It also teaches that divine condemnation is not arbitrary; it is the just consequence of persistent disobedience and transgression, a law that applies to all nations, including the Muslim ummah, if they were to tread the same path.",
  "79": "This verse pinpoints the societal sin that accelerated the downfall of the Israelite community, a sin that is as relevant today for any community as it was then. The statement “They did not forbid one another from doing evil” is a succinct diagnosis of a collapsed moral immune system. The community had reached a point where evil was committed openly, brazenly, and no one had the moral courage or the spiritual concern to stand up and say, “Fear Allah; this is wrong.” The duty of mutual admonition, which is the safeguard of any righteous society, was completely abandoned. The elite were silent in exchange for favour, the scholars were silent in exchange for status, and the common people were silent in exchange for a false peace. This collective silence creates an environment where sin is normalised and ultimately becomes the dominant culture. When that happens, the entire community becomes a target for divine punishment, because the good people, by their silence, have implicitly endorsed the evil.\n\nThe verse then delivers the divine evaluation of their actions: “Evil indeed was what they did! ” The word for “evil” here is *labi’sa*, a term of strong condemnation. Their deeds, both the original sins and the sin of failing to forbid them, were utterly vile. This verse is a direct call to action for the Muslim ummah. The Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, said, “Whoever among you sees an evil, let him change it with his hand; if he cannot, then with his tongue; and if he cannot, then with his heart, and that is the weakest of faith. ” The silent spectator of sin, who has the ability to speak but remains quiet, shares in the collective guilt. The lesson of this verse is that a community’s health is not measured merely by the piety of a few individuals but by its capacity to collectively reject and correct wrongdoing. When the voice of correction dies, the community has effectively signed its own death warrant in the spiritual sense, and often in the worldly sense as well.",
  "80": "This verse serves as a concluding remark on the political and social behaviour of the rebellious Jews of Medina, bringing the theme of false guardianship that has run throughout the surah to a sharp close. The Prophet is told, “You see many of them taking the disbelievers as allies.” This refers to the Jews forming political and military alliances with the pagan Quraysh and other idolatrous Arab tribes against the Muslim community. This was a profound betrayal of any shared Abrahamic principle, because they were choosing the polytheists, who worshipped idols, over the believers, who worshipped the one God. The motivation was pure worldly interest and hatred of the Prophet. This act is immediately condemned: “Truly wicked are their misdeeds.\n\n” The word “wicked” translates *bi’sa*, expressing a deep repulsiveness. Their deeds are not just mistaken; they are morally repugnant.\n\nThe verse then explains the direct consequence of these misdeeds: “which have earned them Allah’s wrath.” The phrase “earned them” indicates that their actions were the direct cause, the *sabab*, that made them deserve the divine anger, *ghaḍab*. Allah’s wrath is the removal of His mercy, the state of being distanced from the Lord’s favour, a state that manifests as humiliation in this world and punishment in the next.\n\nThe verse then delivers the final, grave sentence: “And they will be in everlasting torment. ” This affirms the eternal nature of the punishment for those who die upon such a state of active, knowing rebellion and alliance against the truth. It echoes the warning earlier in the surah about those who take the disbelievers as guardians. This verse is a stark warning about the eternal cost of political and social compromises that betray the faith. In every age, believers are tested with the temptation to seek the favour and protection of powers that oppose the truth, justifying it under the guise of pragmatism. The verse declares that such a choice, when it involves active support against the community of faith, is a direct path to divine wrath and eternal loss.",
  "81": "Indeed We sent down Torah in which guidance and light — by it prophets who submitted judged for Jews, and rabbis and scholars by what they were entrusted of Book Allah and they were witnesses — so do not fear people fear Me and do not sell My verses for small price — whoever does not judge by what Allah revealed — those disbelievers.\n\nInna anzalna at-Tawrata fiha hudan wa nur yahkumu biha an-nabiyyuna alladhina aslamu lilladhina hadu war-rabbaniyyuna wal-ahbaru bima istuhfizu min Kitabi Allahi wa kanu alayhi shuhada fa-la takhshaw an-nas wakhshawni wa la tashtaru bi-ayati thamanan qalilan wa man lam yahkum bima anzala Allahu fa-ula'ika hum al-kafirun — Sent down Torah guidance light — prophets submitted judged for Jews, rabbis scholars by what entrusted of Book Allah and they witnesses.\n\nFa-la takhshaw an-nas wakhshawni — Do not fear people fear Me — same as do not fear them fear Me if believers (3:175) and fear Me.\n\nWa la tashtaru bi-ayati thamanan qalilan — Do not sell verses small price — as earlier exchange covenant small price (4:77), cast behind backs small price (3:187), sell guidance error (2:16).\n\nWa man lam yahkum bima anzala Allahu fa-ula'ika hum al-kafirun — Who does not judge by what Allah revealed — disbelievers — true.\n\nTorah guidance light — as earlier light clear Book (5:15) Quran light.\n\nProphets submitted — aslamu — Islam Way.\n\nRabbaniyyun — devoted to Lord — as earlier be rabbaniyyin because teaching Book (3:79).\n\nEntrusted of Book and witnesses — must make clear not hide (3:187).\n\nDo not fear people fear Me.\n\nNext We decreed for them life for life eye for eye.",
  "82": "And We decreed for them life for life, eye for eye, nose for nose, ear for ear, tooth for tooth, and wounds equal retribution — but whoever waives it as charity it will be expiation for him — whoever does not judge by what Allah revealed — those wrongdoers.\n\nWa katabna alayhim fiha anna an-nafsa bin-nafsi wal-ayna bil-ayni wal-anfa bil-anfi wal-udhuna bil-udhuni was-sinna bis-sinni wal-juruha qisas fa-man tasaddaqa bihi fa-huwa kaffaratun lahu wa man lam yahkum bima anzala Allahu fa-ula'ika hum az-zalimun — Decreed life for life eye eye nose nose ear ear tooth tooth wounds qisas.\n\nBut whoever waives as charity expiation for him — tasaddaqa — charity — kaffarah — as earlier pardon is closer righteousness (2:237) and to pardon is closer taqwa.\n\nWho does not judge by what revealed — zalimun — wrongdoers.\n\nQisas life — as earlier life in retribution (2:179) there is life.\n\nEquality shows justice.\n\nWaiving as charity expiation shows mercy within justice.\n\nNext following footsteps We sent Isa son Maryam confirming Torah We gave him Gospel guidance light confirming Torah guidance admonition for mindful.",
  "83": "And following footsteps We sent Isa son Maryam confirming what before him of Torah — We gave him Gospel containing guidance and light confirming what before it of Torah and as guidance and admonition for mindful.\n\nWa qaffayna ala atharihim bi-Isa ibni Maryama musaddiqan lima bayna yadayhi min at-Tawrati wa ataynahu al-Injila fihi hudan wa nur wa musaddiqan lima bayna yadayhi min at-Tawrati wa hudan wa maw'izatan lil-muttaqin — Following footsteps sent Isa confirming Torah before, gave Gospel guidance light confirming Torah guidance admonition for mindful — muttaqin.\n\nQaffayna — following — succession prophets.\n\nMusaddiq — confirming — as earlier Quran confirming before, Torah confirming? Actually Injil confirming Torah.\n\nGuidance light — huda nur — same as Torah.\n\nHuda maw'izah lil-muttaqin — guidance admonition for mindful — as earlier bayan huda maw'izah lil-muttaqin (3:134).\n\nChain Torah, Gospel, Quran all guidance light.\n\nNext let People Gospel judge by what Allah revealed therein — whoever does not judge by what Allah revealed those defiantly disobedient.",
  "84": "And let People of Gospel judge by what Allah revealed in it — whoever does not judge by what revealed — they are defiantly disobedient.\n\nWalyahkum Ahlu al-Injili bima anzala Allahu fihi wa man lam yahkum bima anzala Allahu fa-ula'ika hum al-fasiqun — Let People Gospel judge by what Allah revealed in it — original Gospel not distorted, but later.\n\nWho does not judge — fasiqun — defiantly disobedient — earlier kafirun, zalimun, now fasiqun — three categories for not judging by what Allah revealed — shows severity levels: disbelief, wrongdoing, defiance.\n\nPeople Gospel judged by Gospel includes description Muhammad, tawhid.\n\nNow Quran final judge.\n\nNext We revealed to you Book in truth confirming what before it and guardian over it so judge between them by what Allah revealed and do not follow desires away from truth — for each We made law and method — had Allah willed He would have made you one nation but to test you in what He gave — so race to good deeds — to Allah return all will inform what you differed.",
  "85": "And We revealed to you Book in truth confirming what before it and as guardian over it — so judge between them by what Allah revealed and do not follow their desires away from what came to you of truth — for each We made law and method — had Allah willed He would have made you one nation but to test you in what He gave — race to good deeds — to Allah return all He will inform what you differed.\n\nWa anzalna ilayka al-Kitaba bil-haqqi musaddiqan lima bayna yadayhi min al-Kitabi wa muhayminan alayh fahkum baynahum bima anzala Allahu wa la tattabi' ahwa'ahum amma ja'aka min al-haqq li-kullin ja'alna minkum shir'atan wa minhaja wa law sha'a Allahu la-ja'alakum ummatan wahidatan wa lakin li-yabluwakum fi-ma atakum fa-istabiqul-khayrat ila Allahi marji'ukum jami'an fa-yunabbi'ukum bima kuntum fihi takhtalifun — Book truth confirming before and muhaymin — guardian, criterion, watcher over it — confirms preserved corrects altered.\n\nJudge by what Allah revealed not follow desires away truth.\n\nLi-kullin ja'alna shir'atan wa minhaja — For each community law and method — shir'ah — law, minhaj — method — different laws for different times as earlier Prophets brothers from one father different mothers — one faith differing laws.\n\nLaw sha'a la-ja'alakum ummatan wahidah — Had willed would have made one nation — but to test you — li-yabluwakum — in what gave.\n\nFa-istabiqul-khayrat — Race to good deeds — as earlier race good deeds (2:148) hasten forgiveness (3:130).\n\nTo Allah return He will inform what differed — as earlier Allah will judge between them Day concerning differed (2:113).\n\nMuhaymin concept important: Quran guards earlier Books.\n\nNext judge between them by what Allah revealed and do not follow desires and beware they tempt you away from some what Allah revealed.",
  "86": "And judge between them by what Allah revealed and do not follow their desires and beware they tempt you away from some of what Allah revealed to you — if they turn away know Allah wants afflict them for some sins — many people are defiantly disobedient.\n\nWa ani ahkum baynahum bima anzala Allahu wa la tattabi' ahwa'ahum wahdharhum an yaftinuka an ba'di ma anzala Allahu ilayka fa-in tawallaw fa'lam annama yuridu Allahu an yusibahum bi-ba'di dhunubihim wa inna kathiran min an-nasi la-fasiqun — Judge by what revealed not follow desires beware they tempt — yaftinuka — test you away from some revealed.\n\nIf turn away know Allah wants afflict them for some sins — due to turning away.\n\nMany people fasiqun — defiantly disobedient.\n\nBeware temptation to compromise some revelation to please them seeking common? Earlier never will Jews Christians pleased until follow faith (2:120) and guidance Allah true guidance.\n\nFasiqun many — not few.\n\nNext do they seek judgment of ignorance? Whose judgment better than Allah for people certain?",
  "87": "Do they seek judgment of ignorance? Whose judgment better than Allah for people certain in faith?\n\nAfa-hukma al-jahiliyyati yabghun wa man ahsanu min Allahi hukman li-qawmin yuqinun — Do they seek hukm jahiliyyah — ignorance — jahiliyyah — pre-Islam ignorance judging by desire tribe.\n\nWa man ahsanu min Allahi hukman li-qawmin yuqinun — Whose judgment better than Allah for people certain — yuqinun — sure faith — yaqin — earlier signs clear for people sure faith (2:118, 2:118?).\n\nJahiliyyah judgment vs Allah judgment contrast.\n\nFor people yuqinun, Allah judgment best.\n\nThis verse after not follow desires beware tempt away some revelation — shows alternative to Allah judgment is jahiliyyah.\n\nToday many seek jahiliyyah judgments — secular desires etc.\n\nFor muqin — certain — Allah judgment best.\n\nNext O you who believe do not take Jews Christians as allies some allies others — whoever among you takes them allies is among them — Allah does not guide wrongdoing people.",
  "88": "O you who believe, do not take Jews and Christians as allies — some are allies of others — whoever among you takes them as allies is among them — Allah does not guide wrongdoing people.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu la tattakhidhu al-Yahuda wan-Nasara awliya ba'duhum awliya ba'd wa man yatawallahum minkum fa-innahu minhum InnAllaha la yahdi al-qawma az-zalimin — Do not take Jews Christians as allies — awliya — intimate protectors instead of believers — ba'duhum awliya ba'd — some allies others — they ally each other.\n\nWho among you takes them allies is among them — identifies with them.\n\nAllah does not guide wrongdoing — zalimin — same closing as many.\n\nThis verse similar to not take disbelievers as allies instead believers (3:28) and not take inner circle outside (3:118) and not take disbelievers allies instead believers (4:128). Context: Battle Ahzab? Some hypocrites sought Jewish Christian alliance for protection.\n\nAwliya here means taking as close protectors seeking honour with them — as earlier do they seek honour with them honour belongs Allah entirely (4:127).\n\nSeeking honour via alliance wrong.\n\nNot forbidding kind just treatment (60:8) — food People Book lawful, chaste women lawful, etc — but intimate alliance shaping loyalty against believers prohibited.\n\nFor today, loyalty determines.\n\nNext you see those in hearts disease hastening toward them saying we fear calamity may befall us — perhaps Allah bring victory or matter from Himself so they become regretful of what concealed in selves.",
  "89": "You see those in whose hearts disease hastening toward them saying we fear calamity may strike us — perhaps Allah will bring victory or matter from Himself so they become regretful over what concealed in selves.\n\nFa-tara alladhina fi qulubihim maradun yusari'una fihim yaquluna nakhsha an tusibana da'irah fa-asa Allahu an ya'tiya bil-fathi aw amrin min indihi fa-yusbihu ala ma asarru fi anfusihim nadimin — Those hearts disease — marad — as earlier hypocrites hearts disease increased (2:10) — hastening toward them — yusari'una fihim — racing to ally with Jews Christians saying nakhsha an tusibana da'irah — fear calamity — da'ira — may befall — need protection.\n\nFa-asa Allahu an ya'tiya bil-fathi — Perhaps Allah bring victory — fath — opening, or matter from Himself — amr — so they become nadimin — regretful what concealed — asarru — in selves.\n\nDaira — calamity circle — fear.\n\nThis describes hypocrites in Madinah fearing Muslims lose, so ally with outside to secure if Muslims lose; if Muslims win, they also have cover.\n\nPerhaps Allah bring fath — victory at Khaybar? Or Fath Makkah?\n\nRegretful over what concealed — concealment (3:29) Allah knows.\n\nNext believers say are these those who swore by Allah strongest oaths they were with you — deeds wasted became losers.",
  "90": "Believers say, Are these those who swore by Allah strongest oaths they were with you? Their deeds wasted became losers.\n\nWa yaqulu alladhina amanu a-ha'ula'i alladhina aqsamu billahi jahda aymanihim innahum la-ma'akum habitat a'maluhum fa-asbahu khasirin — Believers say are these those swore strongest oaths they with you? Their deeds wasted — habitat — became losers — khasirin — as earlier losers.\n\nSwore strongest oaths — jahda aymanihim — same as hypocrites swearing we intended only good reconciliation (4:61) and swearing they were with you (5:89?Actually 9:62).\n\nDeeds wasted — as earlier deeds wasted world Hereafter (3:22) — because hidden shirk.\n\nKhasirin — losers — as earlier.\n\nNext O you who believe whoever among you apostatizes from religion — Allah will bring people He loves and who love Him humble toward believers stern toward disbelievers striving path Allah not fearing blame of blamer — that bounty Allah gives whom wills — Allah Vast, Knowing.",
  "91": "O you who believe, whoever among you turns back from religion — Allah will bring people He loves and they love Him — humble toward believers, stern toward disbelievers, striving in Allah path not fearing blame of blamer — that is bounty Allah gives whom wills — Allah Vast, Knowing.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu man yartadda minkum an dinihi fa-sawfa ya'ti Allahu bi-qawmin yuhibbuhum wa yuhibbunahu adhillatin alal-mu'minina a'izzatin alal-kafirin yujahiduna fi sabili Allahi wa la yakhafuna lawmata la'im dhalika fadlu Allahi yu'tihi man yasha Wallahu Wasi'un Alim — Whoever turns back — yartadda — apostatizes from deen.\n\nFa-sawfa ya'ti Allahu bi-qawmin — Allah will bring people — quality: yuhibbuhum wa yuhibbunahu — He loves them they love Him — mutual love — as earlier if you love Allah follow me Allah will love you (3:31).\n\nAdhillatin alal-mu'minina — humble towards believers — not arrogant — a'izzatin alal-kafirin — stern towards disbelievers — firm.\n\nYujahiduna fi sabili Allah wa la yakhafuna lawmata la'im — Strive path Allah not fear blame of blamer — lawmah — blame — of blamer — as earlier fear Me fear not them.\n\nThat is bounty Allah gives whom wills — fadl — as Owner great bounty.\n\nWasi' Alim — Vast Knowing — Wasi' appears around provision bounty.\n\nThis verse after hypocrites hastening, deeds wasted — warns apostasy after belief — Allah will replace with better people who love Him He loves them — humble to believers stern to disbelievers jihad not fear blame.\n\nFor today, replacement sunnah: community that apostatizes replaced.\n\nQualities praised: love mutual, humility to believers, strenght to disbelievers, jihad, no fear blame.\n\nNext only allies are Allah and His Messenger and those believed establish prayer give zakah bowing.",
  "92": "Your allies are only Allah and His Messenger and those who believed — who establish prayer give zakah while bowing.\n\nInnama waliyyukumu Allahu wa Rasuluhu walladhina amanu alladhina yuqimuna as-salata wa yu'tuna az-zakata wa hum raki'un — Only allies — wali — are Allah, Messenger, those believed who establish prayer give zakah while bowing — raki'un — state? Story Ali gave ring while bowing? Some narrations.\n\nRaki'un — bowing — devout? As earlier bow with those bowing (2:43).\n\nAfter warning not take Jews Christians as allies, now who are allies: Allah, Messenger, believers who establish prayer give zakah while bowing — humble.\n\nWaliyy — ally — as earlier Allah Guardian believers (2:257).\n\nEstablish prayer give zakah — as earlier birr, taqwa.\n\nRaki'un — bowing — includes physical and humility.\n\nNext whoever takes Allah and His Messenger and those believed as allies — party of Allah they victorious.",
  "93": "Whoever takes Allah and His Messenger and those who believed as allies — indeed party of Allah — they will be victorious.\n\nWa man yatawalla Allaha wa Rasulahu walladhina amanu fa-inna hizba Allahi hum al-ghalibun — Whoever takes Allah Messenger those believed as allies — allies vs earlier allies Jews Christians as allies — indeed party Allah — hizb Allah — they victorious — ghalibun.\n\nHizb Allah vs Hizb Shaytan — party Satan.\n\nVictorious — ghalibun — as earlier uppermost if true believers (3:135) and Allah will cut off portion disbelievers turn back disappointed, etc.\n\nParty Allah victorious because Allah stronger force more severe deterrence (4:79), Mighty Wise.\n\nAfter prohibition alliance with Jews Christians seeking honour, true honour party Allah victorious.\n\nNext O you who believe do not take as allies those who mock religion — from those given Book before and disbelievers — fear Allah if believers.",
  "94": "O you who believe, do not take as allies those who mock your religion as amusement from those given Book before you and disbelievers — fear Allah if you are believers.\n\nYa ayyuha alladhina amanu la tattakhidhu alladhina ittakhadhu dinakum huzuwan wa la'iban min alladhina utu al-Kitaba min qablikum wal-kuffara awliya wattaqu Allaha in kuntum mu'minina — Do not take as allies those who mock religion as amusement — huzuwan wa la'iban — mockery amusement — from those given Book before and disbelievers.\n\nEarlier 2:114 who does more wrong than prevents Allah's name mentioned and strives destroy places worship — similar mocking.\n\nHuzuwan — jest — as hypocrites said we were only mocking (2:14) and said we believe then alone with devils say we mocking.\n\nWa-ttaqu Allaha in kuntum mu'minina — Fear if believers.\n\nMocking religion not take as close allies seeking honour; humour that belittles deen not tolerated as ally.\n\nNext when you call to prayer they take it mockery amusement — that because they are people who do not reason.",
  "95": "When you call to prayer they take it as mockery and amusement — that because they are people who do not reason.\n\nWa idha nadaytum ila as-salati ittakhadhuha huzuwan wa la'iban dhalika bi-annahum qawmun la ya'qilun — When call to prayer — adhan — they take mockery amusement — huzuwan la'iban — same as previous.\n\nDhalika bi-annahum qawmun la ya'qilun — Because they people not reason — la ya'qilun — as earlier deaf dumb blind not reason (2:18, 2:171) and like livestock more astray.\n\nAdhan mockery shows lack reason, as earlier ra'ina twisting mocked.\n\nPrayer is worship, calling to success — hayya alal-falah — mocking it shows foolishness.\n\nNext Say O People Book do you resent us except because we believe in Allah and what revealed to us and before and most of you defiantly disobedient.",
  "96": "Say, O People of Book, do you resent us except that we believe in Allah and what revealed to us and what revealed before and most of you are defiantly disobedient?\n\nQul ya Ahla al-Kitabi hal tanqimuna minna illa an amanna billahi wa ma unzila ilayna wa ma unzila min qablu wa anna aktharakum fasiqun — Do you resent us except because we believed in Allah and what revealed to us and before and most you fasiqun defiantly disobedient?\n\nHal tanqimuna — resent — naqama — revenge — why resent faith?\n\nWe believe in Allah and what revealed to us and before — same charter no distinction — while you differentiate.\n\nMost you fasiqun — same as most People Book defiantly disobedient (3:108).\n\nResentment not because we did wrong to you but because we believe — as earlier wish mislead because envy after truth clear (2:109).\n\nNext shall I inform you of worse than that recompense with Allah — those cursed Allah angry made apes swine worship Taghut.",
  "97": "Say, shall I inform you of worse than that as recompense with Allah? Those whom Allah cursed and became angry with and made among them apes and swine and worshippers of Taghut — those worse in position and more astray from straight path.\n\nQul hal unabbi'ukum bi-sharrin min dhalika mathubatan inda Allah man la'anahu Allahu wa ghadiba alayhi wa ja'ala minhum al-qiradata wal-khanazira wa abada at-Taghut ula'ika sharrun makanan wa adallu an sawa'i as-sabil — Say shall inform you worse than that recompense — mathubah — recompense — with Allah? Those cursed Allah, angry, made apes swine worship Taghut — those worse position more astray.\n\nMathubah — recompense opposite sawab reward? Evil recompense.\n\nCursed angry made apes swine — refers Sabbath breakers turned monkeys (2:65), perhaps others?\n\nAbada Taghut — worship Taghut — as earlier taghut false gods.\n\nSharrun makanan wa adallu an sawa as-sabil — Worse position more astray.\n\nAfter people resent believers for belief, inform worse: those cursed.\n\nNext when they come to you say we believe while entered disbelief and left with it — Allah knows what they hide.",
  "98": "When they come to you they say we believe while they entered with disbelief and left with it — Allah knows what they hide.\n\nWa idha ja'ukum qalu amanna wa qad dakhalu bil-kufri wa hum qad kharaju bihi Wallahu A'lamu bima kanu yaktumun — Come saying we believe while entered disbelief and left with it — entered with kufr left with kufr — unchanged, hypocrisy.\n\nAllah knows what they hide — yaktumun — same as earlier conceal truth while know, conceal what chests, etc.\n\nYa'lamu — Knows hidden.\n\nThis describes double-faced: come believers with amanna, leave with kufr.\n\nNext you see many of them hastening to sin transgression consuming forbidden — evil is what they used to do.",
  "99": "You see many of them hastening to sin, transgression, and consuming forbidden — evil is what they used to do.\n\nWa tara kathiran minhum yusari'una fil-ithmi wal-udwani wa aklihim as-suht la-bi'sa ma kanu ya'malun — Many hastening to sin transgression consuming forbidden — suht — haram — as earlier devourers forbidden (59) akkaluna lis-suht.\n\nYusari'una fil-ithmi — hastening to sin — as earlier rush to disbelief (3:176, 5:58).\n\nLa-bi'sa ma kanu ya'malun — Evil what they used to do — as earlier evil what they used to do (5:62? Actually 5:66?).\n\nHastening sin shows eagerness.\n\nConsuming forbidden — suht — bribes, riba, etc.\n\nNext why do rabbis and scholars not forbid them evil speech and consuming forbidden — evil what they used to produce.",
  "100": "Why do not rabbis and scholars forbid them evil speech and consuming forbidden? Evil is what they used to produce.\n\nLaw la yanhahum ar-rabbaniyyuna wal-ahbaru an qawlihim al-ithma wa aklihim as-suht la-bi'sa ma kanu yasna'un — Why not rabbis scholars — rabbaniyyun ahbar — forbid evil speech consuming forbidden? Evil what they produce — yasna'un — produce? Yasna — manufacturing? As earlier produce?\n\nRabbaniyyun — devoted to Lord as earlier be rabbaniyyin because teaching Book (3:79) — Ahbar — scholars.\n\nTheir role enjoin right forbid wrong (3:104) — best nation — but they did not forbid.\n\nLa-bi'sa ma kanu yasna'un — Evil what they produce — produce because they facilitate via silence.\n\nEarlier evil what they used to do (99) — ya'malun — do; this evil what they produce — yasna'un — produce as leaders producing permissive environment.\n\nNeglect of forbidding leads community fast sin.\n\nNext Jews say Allah's hand tied, tied are their hands cursed for what said — rather both hands outstretched giving as He wills — let increase rebellion disbelief for many what revealed to you from Lord — We stirred among them enmity hatred till Day Resurrection — whenever they kindle fire war Allah extinguishes — strive corruption earth — Allah does not love corrupters.",
  "101": "Jews say Allah's hand is tied — tied are their hands and cursed for what they said — rather both His hands outstretched giving as He wills — let increase many of them in rebellion and disbelief what revealed to you from Lord — We stirred among them enmity hatred till Day — whenever they kindle fire for war Allah extinguishes it — they strive corruption earth — Allah does not love corrupters.\n\nWa qalat al-Yahudu yadu Allahi maghlulah ghullat aydihim wa lu'inu bima qalu bal yadahu mabsutatan yunfiqu kayfa yasha — Jews said Allah hand tied — stingy — Allah does not give? As earlier Allah poor we rich (3:181).\n\nGhullat aydihim wa lu'inu — Tied are their hands cursed — du'a against.\n\nBal yadahu mabsutatan — Both Hands outstretched giving as wills — yad — Hand attribute affirmed, without how, vast giving as earlier Owner great bounty provides without account.\n\nLa-yazidanna kathiran minhum ma unzila ilayka... tughyanan wa kufran — Will increase many rebellion disbelief what revealed — as earlier increasing rebellion disbelief what revealed (5:64? Actually 5:64 here) — same as earlier Quran increases wrongdoers loss (2:???). Those who deviate follow mutashabih.\n\nWa alqayna baynahum al-adawata wal-baghda ila yawmi al-qiyamati kulla ma awqadu naran lil-harbi atfa'aha Allahu — Stirred enmity hatred till Day, whenever kindle fire war Allah extinguishes — atfa'aha — extinguishes.\n\nYas'awna fil-ardi fasadan Wallahu la yuhibbu al-mufsidin — Strive corruption earth Allah does not love corrupters — mufsidin — as earlier Allah does not love corruption, not love transgressors.\n\nBoth Hands outstretched shows generosity, not tied.\n\nNext had People Book believed been mindful We would have expiated evil deeds admitted to Gardens bliss.",
  "102": "Had People of Book believed and been mindful, We would have expiated their evil deeds and admitted them to Gardens of bliss.\n\nWa law anna Ahla al-Kitabi amanu wattaqaw la-kaffarna anhum sayyi'atihim wa la-adkhalnahum jannati an-na'im — Had believed and been mindful — amanu wattaqaw — believed plus taqwa — expiated evil deeds — kaffarna — admitted Gardens bliss — Na'im.\n\nOpposite earlier severe — now invitation mercy.\n\nAmanu wattaqaw — same as best provision taqwa — condition for kaffarah Gardens.\n\nNext had they established Torah, Gospel and what revealed from Lord they would have eaten from above and below.",
  "103": "Had they established Torah and Gospel and what was revealed to them from Lord, they would have eaten from above them and from beneath their feet — among them is moderate community but many evil is what they do.\n\nWa law annahum aqamu at-Tawrata wal-Injila wa ma unzila ilayhim min Rabbihim la-akalu min fawqihim wa min tahti arjulihim minhum ummatun muqtasidatun wa kathirun minhum sa'a ma ya'malun — Had established Torah Gospel and what revealed from Lord — aqamu — establish — not just read but implement judging by what Allah revealed (81-84) — would have eaten from above and beneath feet — rizq from sky rain and earth crops — abundance.\n\nMinhum ummatun muqtasidatun — Among them moderate community — muqtasidah — middle, balanced, not extreme.\n\nWa kathirun minhum sa'a ma ya'malun — But many evil what they do — sa'a — evil.\n\nEstablishing Torah Gospel would have meant believing Quran confirming? So eating abundance.\n\nModerate community — as earlier upright community reciting night (3:111) — muqtasidah.\n\nMany evil what they do — repeating evil.\n\nNext O Messenger convey what revealed to you from Lord if not you have not conveyed message Allah will protect you from people Allah does not guide disbelieving people.",
  "104": "O Messenger, convey what was revealed to you from Lord — if you do not, you have not conveyed His message — Allah will protect you from people — Allah does not guide disbelieving people.\n\nYa ayyuha ar-Rasul balligh ma unzila ilayka min Rabbika wa in lam taf'al fa-ma ballaghta risalatahu Wallahu ya'simuka min an-nas InnAllaha la yahdi al-qawma al-kafirin — Convey what revealed — balligh — tabligh — duty.\n\nIf not conveyed message not conveyed.\n\nAllah will protect — ya'simuka — from people — protection after conveying — as earlier Allah restrained hands.\n\nAllah does not guide disbelieving people — same as many endings.\n\nThis verse after moderate community — now command to Messenger to convey despite fear.\n\nBalligh includes conveying Ayat about Isa not god, about first House, about common word — despite dislike.\n\nProtection assured.\n\nNext Say O People Book you are upon nothing until you establish Torah Gospel and what revealed to you from Lord — will increase many rebellion disbelief what revealed to you — do not grieve over disbelieving people.",
  "105": "Say, O People of Book, you stand on nothing until you establish Torah and Gospel and what was revealed to you from Lord — what revealed to you from Lord will increase many of them in rebellion and disbelief — so do not grieve over disbelieving people.\n\nQul ya Ahla al-Kitabi lastum ala shay'in hatta tuqimu at-Tawrata wal-Injila wa ma unzila ilaykum min Rabbikum wa la-yazidanna kathiran minhum ma unzila ilayka min Rabbika tughyanan wa kufran fa-la ta'sa ala al-qawmi al-kafirin — You stand on nothing until establish Torah Gospel and what revealed — aqimu — as previous — lastum ala shay'in — upon nothing — no foundation.\n\nWhat revealed will increase many rebellion disbelief — as earlier increases (101).\n\nFa-la ta'sa ala al-qawmi al-kafirin — Do not grieve over disbelieving people — la ta'sa — similar to do not grieve over fasiqin (5:44) and do not grieve rushers disbelief (3:176).\n\nStanding on nothing — without establishing, religion empty claim.\n\nEstablishing Torah Gospel includes believing Quran because confirms? So standing on nothing until includes Quran.\n\nNext those who believed Jews, Sabeans, Christians whoever believed Allah Last Day and did righteous no fear grieve.",
  "106": "Those who believed, and Jews, Sabeans, Christians — whoever believed in Allah and Last Day and did righteous — no fear upon them nor will they grieve.\n\nInna alladhina amanu walladhina hadu was-sabi'una wan-nasara man amana billahi wal-yawmi al-akhir wa amila salihan fa-la khawfun alayhim wa la hum yahzanun — Similar to 2:62 — Those believed, Jews, Sabeans, Christians — whoever believed Allah Last Day righteous — no fear grieve.\n\nMan amana — whoever believed — includes those before Muhammad who followed their prophet sincerely.\n\nNo fear upon them nor grieve — same phrase no fear grieve for believers many times.\n\nThis shows salvation not by label but belief Allah Last Day righteous.\n\nAfter saying you stand on nothing until establish, this clarifies not exclusive tribal — whoever meets criteria no fear.\n\nBut after final Messenger comes, belief includes believing him (as earlier covenant).\n\nNext We took covenant Children Israel sent messengers — whenever messenger came with what selves did not desire group denied group killed.",
  "107": "We took covenant from Children Israel and sent messengers — whenever messenger came with what selves did not desire — a group they denied and group they killed.\n\nLaqad akhadhna mithaqa Bani Isra'ila wa arsalna ilayhim rusulan kullama ja'ahum rasulun bima la tahwa anfusuhum fariqan kadhdhabu wa fariqan yaqtulun — Covenant Children Israel sent messengers — whenever messenger came with what selves not desire — la tahwa anfusuhum — desires — group denied — kadhdhabu — group killed — yaqtulun — as earlier killing prophets unjustly, etc.\n\nTahwa — desire — follows earlier followers desires want deviate great deviation (26), desire.\n\nMessengers came with what selves not desire — truth often against desire.\n\nDenying killing pattern.\n\nNext they thought no trial would come so blind deaf then Allah turned to them then blind deaf many — Allah Seeing what they do.",
  "108": "They thought no trial would befall so they became blind and deaf — then Allah turned to them — then many of them became blind and deaf again — Allah Seeing what they do.\n\nWa hasibu alla takuna fitnatun fa-'amu wa sammu thumma taba Allahu alayhim thumma 'amu wa sammu kathirun minhum Wallahu Basirun bima ya'malun — Thought no fitnah — trial, punishment — would befall so blind deaf — 'amu sammu — blind deaf — as earlier deaf dumb blind not reason (2:18).\n\nThen Allah turned to them — taba — pardon after?\n\nThen many blind deaf again — repeated.\n\nWallahu Basirun bima ya'malun — Seeing what they do — Basir.\n\nFitnah thought no trial — complacency.\n\nBlind deaf metaphor for not using faculties.\n\nAllah Seeing what they do — knows.\n\nNext certainly disbelieved those who said Allah is Messiah son Maryam while Messiah said O Children Israel worship Allah my Lord your Lord — whoever associates with Allah — Allah forbidden him Paradise refuge Fire — wrongdoers no helpers.",
  "109": "Certainly disbelieved those who said Allah is Messiah son Maryam while Messiah said, O Children Israel worship Allah my Lord and your Lord — whoever associates with Allah Allah forbidden Paradise refuge Fire — wrongdoers no helpers.\n\nLaqad kafara alladhina qalu inna Allaha huwa al-Masihu ibnu Maryama wa qala al-Masihu ya Bani Isra'ila u'budu Allaha Rabbi wa Rabbakum innahu man yushrik billahi fa-qad harrama Allahu alayhi al-jannata wa ma'wahu an-nar wa ma liz-zalimin min ansar — Disbelieved said Allah is Messiah son Maryam — while Messiah said O Children Israel worship Allah my Lord your Lord — as earlier (3:51).\n\nMan yushrik billah fa-qad harrama Allahu alayhi al-jannata — Who associates with Allah forbidden Paradise — haram — refuge Fire.\n\nWa ma liz-zalimin min ansar — No helpers — as earlier no helpers fuel Fire, no helpers disbelievers.\n\nMessiah himself calling worship Allah my Lord your Lord — tawhid.\n\nAssociating Allah forbids Paradise.\n\nNext certainly disbelieved those who said Allah third of three — there is no god except One God — if not desist what say painful punishment will afflict disbelievers.",
  "110": "Certainly disbelieved those who said Allah is third of three — there is no god except One God — if they do not desist from what they say painful punishment will afflict disbelievers among them.\n\nLaqad kafara alladhina qalu inna Allaha thalithu thalathatin wa ma min ilahin illa ilahun wahid wa in lam yantahu amma yaquluna la-yamassanna alladhina kafaru minhum adhabun alim — Third of three — Trinity.\n\nWa ma min ilahin illa ilahun wahid — No god except One God — as earlier no god except Him (3:2).\n\nIf not desist — yantahu — from saying third of three — painful punishment will afflict disbelievers among them.\n\nAfin — willingness to desist.\n\nThalithu thalathah — doctrine Trinity.\n\nOne God tawhid.\n\nNext will they not turn to Allah seek forgiveness — Allah Forgiving, Merciful — Messiah son Maryam only messenger messengers passed before mother truthful both ate food — see how We make clear signs then see how they deluded.",
  "111": "Will they not turn to Allah and seek forgiveness? Allah Forgiving, Merciful — Messiah son Maryam only messenger — messengers passed before him — mother truthful — both ate food — see how We make clear signs — then see how deluded.\n\nAfala yatubuna ila Allahi wa yastaghfirunah Wallahu Ghafur Rahim — Will they not repent to Allah seek forgiveness?\n\nAl-Masihu ibnu Maryama illa rasulun qad khalat min qablihi ar-rusul wa ummuhu siddiqatun kana ya'kulani at-ta'am unzur kayfa nubayyinu lahum al-ayat thumma unzur anna yu'fakun — Messiah only messenger messengers passed before, mother siddiqah — truthful — both ate food — ya'kulani at-ta'am — eating food needs digestion, shows human need, cannot be god — simple proof.\n\nUnzur kayfa nubayyinu lahum al-ayat thumma unzur anna yu'fakun — Look how make clear signs then see how deluded — yu'fakun — turned away deluded.\n\nGhfur Rahim — Forgiving Merciful — door open if turn seek forgiveness.\n\nEating food proof is everyday: god does not need food, but Messiah and mother ate food — needs — as earlier (2:??) creation.\n\nSee how clear signs then deluded.\n\nNext Say do you worship besides Allah what possesses neither harm nor benefit for you — Allah Hearing, Knowing.",
  "112": "Say, do you worship besides Allah what possesses neither harm nor benefit — Allah Hearing, Knowing.\n\nQul a-ta'buduna min duni Allahi ma la yamliku lakum darran wa la naf'an Wallahu Huwa as-Sami'u al-Alim — Worship besides Allah what possesses neither harm nor benefit — darr naf' — harm benefit — neither.\n\nAllah Hearing Knowing — Sami' Alim — hears worship, knows.\n\nWhat besides Allah — idols, Messiah, saints — cannot harm benefit.\n\nWorship besides Allah includes calling Messiah as god — he cannot harm benefit without permission.\n\nHearing Knowing — as earlier nothing hidden, conceal reveal knows.\n\nNext Say O People Book do not exceed in religion beyond truth nor follow desires of people who went astray before and led many astray and strayed from even way.",
  "113": "Say, O People of Book, do not exceed in religion beyond truth nor follow desires of people who went astray before and led many astray and strayed from even way.\n\nQul ya Ahla al-Kitabi la taghlu fi dinikum ghayra al-haqq wa la tattabi'u ahwa'a qawmin qad dallu min qablu wa adallu kathiran wa dallu an sawa'i as-sabil — Do not exceed religion beyond truth — la taghlu — same as earlier do not exceed in religion say not about Allah except truth Messiah only messenger (154 in chapter 4) — ghulu.\n\nDo not follow desires people went astray before led many astray strayed even way — ahwa — desires as earlier followers desires want deviate great (26).\n\nExceed beyond truth includes deifying Isa, saying Allah poor, claiming children beloved, etc.\n\nDesires people went astray before — previous deviant theologians.\n\nSawa as-sabil — even way — straight path.\n\nNext cursed were those disbelieved among Children Israel by tongue Dawud and Isa son Maryam — because disobeyed and transgressed.",
  "114": "Cursed were those who disbelieved among Children Israel by tongue of Dawud and Isa son Maryam — because they disobeyed and transgressed.\n\nLu'ina alladhina kafaru min Bani Isra'il ala lisani Dawuda wa Isa ibni Maryama dhalika bima asaw wa kanu ya'tadun — Cursed disbelieved among Bani Israel by tongue Dawud and Isa — language? Dua curse.\n\nBima asaw wa kanu ya'tadun — Because disobeyed and transgressed — asaw ya'tadun — same as earlier disgrace struck because disobeyed transgressed (110).\n\nCurse by Dawud and Isa — prophets they claimed follow cursed them.\n\nNext they would not forbid one another from wrong they did — evil was what they used to do.",
  "115": "They would not forbid one another from wrong they did — evil was what they used to do.\n\nKanu la yatanahawna an munkarin fa'aluh la-bi'sa ma kanu yaf'alun — Would not forbid one another munkar — wrong they did — la-bi'sa ma kanu yaf'alun — Evil what they used to do — as earlier evil what they used to do (99,100) — la-bi'sa.\n\nNot forbidding munkar leads curse — as best nation enjoin right forbid wrong (3:108,110), letting be among you ummah inviting good (3:104). They abandoned.\n\nLa yatanahawna — not forbid one another.\n\nEvil what they used to do.\n\nNext you see many of them taking disbelievers as allies — evil what souls sent forth Allah angry with them in punishment abiding.",
  "116": "You see many of them taking disbelievers as allies — evil is what they sent forth for themselves — Allah angry with them and in punishment abiding.\n\nTara kathiran minhum yatawallawna alladhina kafaru la-bi'sa ma qaddamat lahum anfusuhum an sakhita Allahu alayhim wa fil-adhahi hum khalidun — Many taking disbelievers allies — yatawallawna — same as taking Jews Christians allies (88) — do they seek honour with them honour belongs Allah (4:127).\n\nLa-bi'sa ma qaddamat lahum anfusuhum — Evil what souls sent forth — qaddamat — sent forth earlier — whatever good you send forth find with Allah (2:110) — evil sent forth also finds.\n\nSakhita Allahu alayhim — Allah angry with them — as earlier those disbelieved Allah cursed angry made apes swine worship Taghut (97).\n\nFil-adhahi hum khalidun — Punishment abiding.\n\nTaking allies disbelievers leads anger.\n\nNext had they believed in Allah and Prophet and what revealed to him they would not have taken them allies but many are defiantly disobedient.",
  "117": "Had they believed in Allah and Prophet and what was revealed to him they would not have taken them as allies — but many of them are defiantly disobedient.\n\nWa law kanu yu'minuna billahi wan-Nabiyyi wa ma unzila ilayhi ma ittakhadhuhum awliya wa lakinna kathiran minhum fasiqun — Had believed Allah Prophet what revealed they would not have taken them allies — true belief prevents alliance seeking honour with disbelievers mocking religion.\n\nBut many fasiqun — defiantly disobedient as earlier most People Book fasiqun (3:108).\n\nFasiq — steps outside.\n\nBelief prevents alliance — as earlier allies only Allah Messenger those believed establish prayer give zakah bowing (5:92) party Allah victorious.\n\nNext you will find strongest in enmity toward believers Jews and polytheists you will find nearest in affection those who said we are Christians — because among them priests monks and they are not arrogant.",
  "118": "You will find strongest in enmity toward believers Jews and polytheists — you will find nearest in affection to believers those who said we are Christians — because among them priests and monks and they are not arrogant.\n\nLa-tajidanna ashadda an-nasi adawatan lilladhina amanu al-Yahuda walladhina ashraku wa la-tajidanna aqrabahum mawaddatan lilladhina amanu alladhina qalu inna nasara dhalika bi-anna minhum qissisina wa ruhbana wa annahum la yastakbirun — Strongest enmity Jews polytheists — nearest affection Christians who said we are Christians — because among them qissisin priests ruhban monks not arrogant — la yastakbirun — not arrogant — as earlier those who disdain arrogant (155 in chap4) and arrogant boastful not loved.\n\nPriests monks ascetic not arrogant closer to accept truth.\n\nHistorical: Najashi Christian king in Abyssinia protected Muslims, wept when heard Quran.\n\nEnmity polytheists Jews due to envy knowledge — as earlier envy.\n\nAffection Christians due humility lack arrogance.\n\nNext when they hear what revealed to Messenger you see eyes overflowing tears because they recognized truth they say Our Lord we believe write us among witnesses.",
  "119": "When they hear what was revealed to Messenger you see eyes overflowing tears because they recognized truth — they say Our Lord we believe write us among witnesses.\n\nWa idha sami'u ma unzila ila ar-Rasuli tara a'yunahum tafidu min ad-dam'i mimma arafu min al-haqq yaquluna Rabbana amanna faktubna ma'a ash-shahidin — When hear what revealed Messenger eyes overflow tears because recognized truth — a'yunahum tafidu min ad-dam'i — eyes flooding tears — from what they recognized of haqq.\n\nYaquluna Rabbana amanna faktubna ma'a ash-shahidin — Our Lord we believe write us among witnesses — same dua hawariyyun write us among witnesses (3:53) and those heard caller faith (3:195?).\n\nEyes overflowing tears sign sincerity — known truth.\n\nMinma arafu min al-haqq — because recognized truth — ya'rifun.\n\nThis describes Najashi companions when heard Quran wept.\n\nRecognition truth causes tears — as earlier upright community reciting verses night prostrate.\n\nWrite us among witnesses — as earlier Allah witnesses no god except Him angels people knowledge, and Messenger witness over these.\n\nNext why should we not believe in Allah and what came to us of truth while we hope Lord will admit us among righteous.",
  "120": "Why should we not believe in Allah and what came to us of truth while we hope our Lord will admit us among righteous people? So Allah rewarded them for what they said Gardens beneath rivers abiding therein — reward of doers good.\n\nWa ma lana la nu'minu billahi wa ma ja'ana min al-haqq wa natma'u an yudkhilana Rabbuna ma'a al-qawmi as-salihin — Why not believe in Allah and what came us truth while hope Lord admit among righteous — qawmi salihin — righteous — salihin as earlier Yahya Isa among salihin.\n\nHope admit among righteous — natma'u — hope.\n\nFa-athabahumu Allahu bima qalu jannatin tajri min tahtiha al-anhar khalidina fiha wa dhalika jaza'u al-muhsinin — So Allah rewarded them for what they said — bima qalu — for what they said — Gardens rivers abiding — reward doers good — muhsinin — as earlier Allah loves doers good.\n\nBima qalu — for what they said — speech: Rabbana amanna faktubna — rewarded Gardens.\n\nMukhlisin? Reward doers good.\n\nCloses Ma'idah beginning section about covenants, food lawful, People Book, enmity affection, tears recognition.\n\nFrom fulfilling contracts, lawful tayyibat, food People Book, chaste women, wudu purification, covenant, do not fear people fear Me, do not sell verses small price, Torah guidance light, life for life, Gospel guidance, Quran guardian, race good deeds, do not take Jews Christians allies seeking honour, honour belongs Allah entirely, those hearts disease hastening, believers swore, Allah will bring people He loves they love Him humble believers stern disbelievers, allies only Allah Messenger believers, do not take mockers allies, call prayer mockery they not reason, resent us except believe, worse recompense cursed angry apes swine Taghut worship, when come say we believe entered disbelief left Allah knows hide, hastening sin transgression consuming forbidden, why rabbis scholars not forbid evil speech consuming forbidden, Jews say hand tied both hands outstretched, had People Book believed mindful expiated admitted bliss, had established Torah Gospel eating above beneath moderate community many evil, O Messenger convey what revealed Allah protect you, People Book stand on nothing until establish Torah Gospel what revealed, those believed Jews Sabeans Christians whoever believed Allah Last Day righteous no fear grieve, covenant Children Israel whenever messenger came what selves not desire group denied group killed, thought no trial blind deaf then turned then blind deaf Allah Seeing, disbelieved said Allah is Messiah Messiah said worship Allah my Lord your Lord whoever associates forbidden Paradise refuge Fire, disbelieved said Allah third of three no god except One if not desist painful afflict, will they not turn seek forgiveness Forgiving Merciful Messiah only messenger mother truthful both ate food see how clear signs deluded, worship besides Allah neither harm benefit Allah Hearing Knowing, People Book do not exceed beyond truth not follow desires strayed before led many astray strayed even way, cursed disbelieved Bani Israel by tongue Dawud Isa because disobeyed transgressed, would not forbid one another wrong evil what they used to do, many taking disbelievers allies evil what sent forth Allah angry punishment abiding, had believed Allah Prophet what revealed not taken allies many fasiqun, find strongest enmity Jews polytheists nearest affection Christians because priests monks not arrogant, when hear what revealed eyes overflowing tears recognition saying Our Lord we believe write among witnesses, why not believe truth hope admit among righteous so Allah rewarded for what they said Gardens reward doers good.\n\nThus chapter Ma'idah speaks."
};
